WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:08.190
- [Prof. Shore] Thank you all for coming,

00:00:08.190 --> 00:00:10.020
I'm very happy to see you.

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:14.070
I am delighted to be introducing
my colleague from Ukraine,

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:17.250
her name is Oksana Mikheieva,

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:21.180
who has come here from the
other side of the ocean,

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:24.030
despite difficult circumstances.

00:00:24.030 --> 00:00:26.340
She comes to us originally from Donetsk,

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:29.460
but in the meantime from the
Ukrainian Catholic University

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:31.890
in Lviv and from the European University

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:33.633
in Viadrina in Germany.

00:00:35.010 --> 00:00:37.680
She is a historian and a sociologist

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:40.383
who combines those methods in her work.

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:43.080
She has been working now

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:45.030
and for the past several years,

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:48.930
or at least since we met
in Vienna a few years ago,

00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:50.730
on this issue of refugees

00:00:50.730 --> 00:00:53.160
and internally displaced persons.

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:55.290
I think most of you may know,

00:00:55.290 --> 00:00:56.850
and Jenu is not in the room

00:00:56.850 --> 00:00:59.700
otherwise he should really
introduce this topic.

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:04.700
So when the war in the
Donbas began in 2014,

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:07.110
it created a situation

00:01:07.110 --> 00:01:09.450
in which not only were
people being slaughtered

00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:12.783
and, even before this
full scale invasion began,

00:01:13.748 --> 00:01:16.470
some 15,000 people had died?

00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:18.330
I may have that number wrong.

00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:20.280
And between a million and a half

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:24.180
and 2 million internally
displaced persons.

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:27.120
And that's a category
that refers to people

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:30.930
who became refugees in their own country.

00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:35.643
So domestic refugees, so
Ukrainian residents of the Donbas,

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:39.150
this post-industrial mining region

00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:40.530
in the far east of Ukraine

00:01:40.530 --> 00:01:42.360
where the war was concentrated,

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:44.430
who were forced to flee their homes

00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:47.010
and go into other parts of Ukraine.

00:01:47.010 --> 00:01:50.250
So it's conceptually, politically,

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:52.233
legally, sociologically,

00:01:53.550 --> 00:01:56.850
you know, it's a very
interesting situation,

00:01:56.850 --> 00:01:59.736
It's a very painful situation,

00:01:59.736 --> 00:02:01.540
It's a kind of agonizing situation

00:02:02.506 --> 00:02:05.370
and Oksana has been quite heroic

00:02:05.370 --> 00:02:09.660
in both being a participant
and observer in those events

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:12.090
and continuing her research

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:15.723
despite the most gruesome circumstances.

00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:18.990
I will not take up any more of her time.

00:02:18.990 --> 00:02:20.400
I just wanted to give those of you

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:22.320
who don't work on Ukraine in particular,

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:24.497
that context of internally
displaced persons,

00:02:24.497 --> 00:02:27.360
'cause I know a lot of you have experience

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:29.580
thinking about and working with refugees

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:31.500
and what does it mean
when you have refugees

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:33.930
who are in their own country,

00:02:33.930 --> 00:02:35.760
but they are homeless and displaced.

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:38.700
What does that mean
for their legal status?

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:41.640
And what does that mean for their lives?

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:44.967
With that, I will turn you
over to Professor Mikheieva

00:02:44.967 --> 00:02:46.893
and thank you so much for coming!

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:49.740
- Thank you very much
for this introductions,

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:53.022
and first of all, I would like to say that

00:02:53.022 --> 00:02:55.680
I am grateful for the
opportunity to be here.

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:59.580
It is a privilege to be in this wonderful

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:02.820
and safe place at a time
when people in Ukraine

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:06.120
are living under full-scale
Russian aggression.

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:07.980
Today I will talk about ordinary people

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:09.603
under extreme life conditions

00:03:09.603 --> 00:03:11.790
and, my main focus was internal,

00:03:11.790 --> 00:03:13.560
and thank you very much
for this introduction.

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:16.591
I really talk about refugees
in their own country

00:03:16.591 --> 00:03:19.290
it's a very good formula
to describe this situation,

00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:22.380
but we are talk about
internally displaced person

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:24.510
in this case, and little bit I say about

00:03:24.510 --> 00:03:26.640
external forced migration from Ukraine

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:29.910
in this time from war torn
territories in Ukraine,

00:03:29.910 --> 00:03:31.707
and what is very important to me

00:03:31.707 --> 00:03:33.903
and to start with you to talk about this,

00:03:33.903 --> 00:03:35.880
that in my presentation

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:38.760
I will talk about the experience
of forced displacement

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:42.060
of residents from the war
torn territories of Ukraine

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:45.840
after 2014, and before
the start of full scale

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:49.433
aggression against Ukraine on
the 24th of February, 2022.

00:03:50.325 --> 00:03:53.040
So important for me because
now we have a complete period.

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:54.060
It's one stage.

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:57.090
It's one stage which have a date of start

00:03:57.090 --> 00:03:59.580
and date of the end.

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:00.953
We are talking about this period

00:04:00.953 --> 00:04:05.373
and try to understand what
lessons we have from this time.

00:04:07.483 --> 00:04:10.390
What took place in
Ukraine from Spring 2014

00:04:10.390 --> 00:04:13.230
to 24 February 2022
can today be classified

00:04:13.230 --> 00:04:14.850
as the first phase of Russian aggression

00:04:14.850 --> 00:04:16.461
against the Ukraine.

00:04:16.461 --> 00:04:19.470
The outbreak of full-scale
war marked the beginning

00:04:19.470 --> 00:04:21.030
of a radically new phase

00:04:21.030 --> 00:04:24.480
which entailed a number
changes in Ukraine.

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:28.050
The other very important point for me

00:04:28.050 --> 00:04:30.510
because we see how these runs

00:04:30.510 --> 00:04:34.230
push Ukrainian society to
do something absolutely new.

00:04:34.230 --> 00:04:36.750
However, these changes largely took shape

00:04:36.750 --> 00:04:39.878
and manifested themselves
during this first

00:04:39.878 --> 00:04:41.820
phase of Russian aggression.

00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:44.010
What are the main developments

00:04:44.010 --> 00:04:46.440
and trends I'm referring to?

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:50.970
While in 2014 we spoke
cautiously about the formation

00:04:50.970 --> 00:04:52.530
of a political Ukrainian nation,

00:04:52.530 --> 00:04:54.380
today Ukrainians, as a political nation,

00:04:54.380 --> 00:04:56.850
are showing themselves quite clearly.

00:04:56.850 --> 00:05:00.690
My separate research devoted
to study this dynamic

00:05:00.690 --> 00:05:03.540
of Ukrainian national identity

00:05:03.540 --> 00:05:06.270
and I see this process as
it's really very complicated

00:05:06.270 --> 00:05:09.320
process for many Ukrainians
because on the one hand,

00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:12.300
we see how people step-by-step

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:15.240
create this modern concept of nation

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:18.480
which are based on citizen,
person holding citizenship.

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:20.613
But, this understanding of citizenship

00:05:20.613 --> 00:05:23.630
still very problematic
from the point of view

00:05:23.630 --> 00:05:25.050
of current situation in Ukraine,

00:05:25.050 --> 00:05:27.330
but is separate topic,
very interesting topic.

00:05:27.330 --> 00:05:29.820
And, we try to study this element

00:05:29.820 --> 00:05:32.141
of citizenship from the point of view

00:05:32.141 --> 00:05:35.400
of ordinary citizenship

00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:37.770
from the hierarchical citizenship,

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:40.800
from the emotional
citizenship (indistinct),

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:43.320
all of these give us a very
interesting information

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:44.583
how it really,

00:05:46.110 --> 00:05:48.183
is now in Ukraine.

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:52.500
The country is experiencing
rapid Ukrainianisation

00:05:52.500 --> 00:05:56.130
even by ear, the Ukrainian
language is perceived

00:05:56.130 --> 00:05:58.200
not only as a language of communication

00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:01.230
but also as a way of distancing

00:06:01.230 --> 00:06:03.840
and protecting oneself from the aggressor.

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:06.677
It's absolutely new
function of the language

00:06:06.677 --> 00:06:07.510
in the country.

00:06:07.510 --> 00:06:09.900
According to the latest results of surveys

00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:12.908
by the Kyiv International
Institute of Sociology,

00:06:12.908 --> 00:06:15.633
so maybe you noticed that
usually it's very good

00:06:15.633 --> 00:06:17.174
the Sociology Institute in the Ukraine,

00:06:17.174 --> 00:06:20.000
despite the horrors of this war,

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:24.670
70% of Ukrainians are
hopeful about the future

00:06:24.670 --> 00:06:29.670
and it's absolutely incredible results.

00:06:30.300 --> 00:06:32.230
The next important moment, Euromaidan.

00:06:32.230 --> 00:06:35.220
And first wave of Russian
aggression was indeed

00:06:35.220 --> 00:06:39.230
a very strong catalyst for
volunteerism in Ukraine.

00:06:39.230 --> 00:06:42.150
If before that participation
in civic organization

00:06:42.150 --> 00:06:43.470
for a long period of time

00:06:43.470 --> 00:06:48.090
was kept on the level of 11 to 14%,

00:06:48.090 --> 00:06:50.550
today we see a completely
different situation.

00:06:50.550 --> 00:06:52.050
According to the survey conducted

00:06:52.050 --> 00:06:54.210
by sociological group Rating,

00:06:54.210 --> 00:06:58.200
in March 2022, more
than 80% of respondents

00:06:58.200 --> 00:06:59.670
indicated that they help

00:06:59.670 --> 00:07:04.493
to defend Ukraine in one
form or another, 80%.

00:07:06.767 --> 00:07:10.753
39% help people and
the army as volunteers,

00:07:10.753 --> 00:07:13.500
37% help financially,

00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:16.530
18% take part in informational resistance,

00:07:16.530 --> 00:07:20.040
12% continue working in
the critical infrastructure

00:07:20.040 --> 00:07:23.566
and only 19% of these
respondents indicated

00:07:23.566 --> 00:07:25.170
that they did not have the opportunity

00:07:25.170 --> 00:07:27.360
to participate in volunteer activities.

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:30.099
There are slightly more
elderly people in this group

00:07:30.099 --> 00:07:31.480
but even in this group

00:07:31.480 --> 00:07:33.930
there are many people who participate

00:07:33.930 --> 00:07:35.583
in volunteering activities.

00:07:36.660 --> 00:07:39.873
It would seem that we now have everything

00:07:39.873 --> 00:07:43.410
that 30 years of Ukrainian
Independence have discussed.

00:07:43.410 --> 00:07:45.060
But what is experience behind it?

00:07:45.060 --> 00:07:47.220
All this marks the situation in Ukraine

00:07:47.220 --> 00:07:49.200
during and after Euromaidan

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:50.450
and in the time of the first period

00:07:50.450 --> 00:07:52.170
of Russian aggression against Ukraine

00:07:52.170 --> 00:07:57.000
from 2014 to 2022 extremely
important to study,

00:07:57.000 --> 00:07:59.250
and now I start my presentation!

00:07:59.250 --> 00:08:00.561
(audience laughs)

00:08:00.561 --> 00:08:04.311
(Oksana speaks indistinctly)

00:08:05.927 --> 00:08:06.830
One second, mhm!

00:08:06.830 --> 00:08:09.580
On this slide you can see titles of,

00:08:09.580 --> 00:08:11.310
and very brief information

00:08:11.310 --> 00:08:14.310
about our main service in this time.

00:08:14.310 --> 00:08:18.780
As you can see it's
really, many of research.

00:08:18.780 --> 00:08:21.330
I will not describe all
these studies in detail

00:08:21.330 --> 00:08:22.680
because we have no time for this,

00:08:22.680 --> 00:08:24.480
only the one important moment.

00:08:24.480 --> 00:08:27.810
We have had the opportunity
to collect data,

00:08:27.810 --> 00:08:32.220
every year, from the
beginning, from 2014 to 2018.

00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:37.380
In most cases it was
a qualitative methods.

00:08:37.380 --> 00:08:39.150
We conducted in-depth interview

00:08:39.150 --> 00:08:42.333
with different group of
people affected by the war.

00:08:47.310 --> 00:08:50.010
Let me start our conversation
about displacement

00:08:50.010 --> 00:08:52.179
by giving a little context.

00:08:52.179 --> 00:08:56.680
From the 20th of February 2014

00:08:56.680 --> 00:08:58.863
to the 24th of February 2022,

00:09:00.090 --> 00:09:02.790
as I says before, it's
dates of the first period

00:09:02.790 --> 00:09:04.560
of Russian aggression against Ukraine.

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:06.300
Within this wave of Russian aggression

00:09:06.300 --> 00:09:10.860
we see two separate cases
that differ significantly.

00:09:10.860 --> 00:09:14.550
First the "soft" also called "bloodless",

00:09:14.550 --> 00:09:17.760
without use weapon, occupation
of Crimean Peninsula

00:09:17.760 --> 00:09:20.220
subsequently led to its annexation

00:09:20.220 --> 00:09:22.470
and incorporation into
the Russian Federation

00:09:22.470 --> 00:09:24.570
as a separate federal district.

00:09:24.570 --> 00:09:27.237
In this case, we have 6 killed people

00:09:27.237 --> 00:09:29.430
and 10 disappear.

00:09:29.430 --> 00:09:31.980
And second, the Russian invasion

00:09:31.980 --> 00:09:35.040
of parts of Donetsk and
Luhansk region, accompanied

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:40.040
by simulated internal civil
conflict and referendums.

00:09:41.524 --> 00:09:42.357
Why is so important now?

00:09:42.357 --> 00:09:46.770
Because now we see the
repeating of such technology

00:09:46.770 --> 00:09:50.493
in Ukraine, but in other
occupied territories.

00:09:54.720 --> 00:09:58.260
All of this led to formation
of quasi-state entities

00:09:58.260 --> 00:10:01.783
so called "Donetsk People's Republic"

00:10:01.783 --> 00:10:04.530
and "Luhansk People's
Republic" on these territories.

00:10:04.530 --> 00:10:06.090
Terminology is other issue,

00:10:06.090 --> 00:10:08.640
very important and I hope,
after my presentation,

00:10:08.640 --> 00:10:12.540
we come back to all of
these terms which are used.

00:10:12.540 --> 00:10:14.970
From my point of view it's very

00:10:14.970 --> 00:10:17.010
big issue for all researchers.

00:10:17.010 --> 00:10:19.590
For example, if you conduct research

00:10:19.590 --> 00:10:22.863
and involves your participants
which are representatives

00:10:22.863 --> 00:10:25.020
from the both side of the conflict,

00:10:25.020 --> 00:10:27.957
it's very important to have
a good language setting,

00:10:27.957 --> 00:10:30.600
how to talk with people,
what terminology to use.

00:10:30.600 --> 00:10:32.792
It's absolutely impossible
to talk with people

00:10:32.792 --> 00:10:35.131
who live on the premium
controlled territory

00:10:35.131 --> 00:10:38.002
using, for example, "DPR",
"LPR", et cetera, et cetera.

00:10:38.002 --> 00:10:39.941
It's absolutely impossible
to talk with people

00:10:39.941 --> 00:10:41.820
who live on occupied territory

00:10:41.820 --> 00:10:44.860
using this terminology
"occupied territory"

00:10:44.860 --> 00:10:47.610
and why it's a very big
issue for researcher

00:10:47.610 --> 00:10:50.250
how to prepare these tools,

00:10:50.250 --> 00:10:51.870
which are very different,

00:10:51.870 --> 00:10:54.780
and how to combine them, this information,

00:10:54.780 --> 00:10:58.343
and make a conclusion if you
ask about something different,

00:10:58.343 --> 00:11:01.950
because these people live
in different reality.

00:11:01.950 --> 00:11:05.760
But, then...

00:11:05.760 --> 00:11:08.490
Well, I go back to this second case.

00:11:08.490 --> 00:11:13.490
In this case, as you can see
we have, so many victims here.

00:11:14.760 --> 00:11:19.760
It's near 44,000 or more than
44,000 losses recorded at all.

00:11:21.240 --> 00:11:22.073
As you can see,

00:11:22.073 --> 00:11:25.650
of those, more than 13,000 killed

00:11:25.650 --> 00:11:28.890
and, more than 30,000 wounded

00:11:28.890 --> 00:11:32.340
23, 25% of this losses were civilians.

00:11:32.340 --> 00:11:34.260
We usually have a problem miscalculating

00:11:34.260 --> 00:11:36.780
the number of victims

00:11:36.780 --> 00:11:39.003
in this case and we
still have this problem,

00:11:39.986 --> 00:11:41.100
why we are using the different number

00:11:41.100 --> 00:11:43.233
when we talk about the situation,

00:11:43.233 --> 00:11:44.847
well it describes the situation.

00:11:44.847 --> 00:11:48.540
As you can see on the next slide,

00:11:48.540 --> 00:11:52.800
most civilian deaths
occurred in 2014-2015,

00:11:52.800 --> 00:11:55.020
during the active phase of hostilities

00:11:55.020 --> 00:11:56.911
in Donetsk and Luhansk oblast.

00:11:56.911 --> 00:11:59.850
This scheme clearly
illustrates the dubiousness

00:11:59.850 --> 00:12:03.120
of Russian theses about
the need to protect

00:12:03.120 --> 00:12:05.561
the so-called "People of Donbas"

00:12:05.561 --> 00:12:10.170
It's next very important
combination of words

00:12:10.170 --> 00:12:12.573
which have very important sounds.

00:12:13.410 --> 00:12:15.030
It's a part of Russian propaganda

00:12:15.030 --> 00:12:19.087
and they try to construct this community,

00:12:19.087 --> 00:12:20.460
"People of Donbas",

00:12:20.460 --> 00:12:23.040
such a community has
never existed in Ukraine;

00:12:23.040 --> 00:12:25.290
moreover, at the level of ordinary people,

00:12:25.290 --> 00:12:30.090
we have recorded active
attempt to deconstruct

00:12:30.090 --> 00:12:32.370
this cliche of Russian propaganda,

00:12:32.370 --> 00:12:34.470
in many of our interviews.

00:12:34.470 --> 00:12:36.503
But let's come back to this
necessity to understand

00:12:36.503 --> 00:12:39.960
the specifics of forced
displacement in this stage

00:12:39.960 --> 00:12:42.990
and the differences in the
conditions of forced migration

00:12:42.990 --> 00:12:45.283
from Crimea and the occupied parts

00:12:45.283 --> 00:12:46.730
of Donetsk and Luhansk oblast.

00:12:46.730 --> 00:12:49.680
In this so called "bloodless" occupation

00:12:49.680 --> 00:12:53.130
of Crimean Peninsula,
people faced pressure,

00:12:53.130 --> 00:12:56.880
a threat primarily because of
their pro-Ukrainian stance.

00:12:56.880 --> 00:12:59.730
The wide range of discriminatory practices

00:12:59.730 --> 00:13:02.700
applied to people who had
pro-Ukrainian positions

00:13:02.700 --> 00:13:04.503
caused their relocation.

00:13:05.580 --> 00:13:07.872
The residents of Donetsk
and Luhansk regions

00:13:07.872 --> 00:13:09.240
fled the war.

00:13:09.240 --> 00:13:12.570
The forced relocation was
caused by a direct threat

00:13:12.570 --> 00:13:15.600
to their life during the
deployment of military action,

00:13:15.600 --> 00:13:18.630
it's a very big difference
with these two contacts.

00:13:18.630 --> 00:13:22.104
Accordingly, they had
very different perception

00:13:22.104 --> 00:13:25.530
of the situation, and did not always

00:13:25.530 --> 00:13:27.420
have a pro-Ukrainian stance.

00:13:27.420 --> 00:13:29.760
What the problem of perception of this

00:13:29.760 --> 00:13:32.130
to do in Ukrainian society,

00:13:32.130 --> 00:13:33.513
but not only this,

00:13:34.887 --> 00:13:39.060
a very important role
played as state rhetoric.

00:13:39.060 --> 00:13:40.080
What I mean,

00:13:40.080 --> 00:13:44.460
in Ukraine this situation
before they issued the law

00:13:44.460 --> 00:13:48.780
in 2018 about this situation.

00:13:48.780 --> 00:13:51.427
We call this situation officially

00:13:51.427 --> 00:13:54.000
"Anti-terroristic operation".

00:13:54.000 --> 00:13:59.000
Only after 2018 we start talking
about war and occupation.

00:14:00.330 --> 00:14:03.210
It's very important for
understanding this context

00:14:03.210 --> 00:14:08.210
because people who are
internally displaced person

00:14:08.970 --> 00:14:13.560
have not a clear statuses and
have no clear understanding

00:14:13.560 --> 00:14:16.915
from why they come to
other part of the country,

00:14:16.915 --> 00:14:19.370
et cetera, et cetera.

00:14:19.370 --> 00:14:21.840
In the following slide, you can see

00:14:21.840 --> 00:14:25.230
the dynamic of registered IDPS in Ukraine

00:14:25.230 --> 00:14:27.750
before the outbreak of full-scale war.

00:14:27.750 --> 00:14:29.070
To show you this dynamic,

00:14:29.070 --> 00:14:30.750
I have used a good visualization

00:14:30.750 --> 00:14:33.720
from the publication
linked to on the slide.

00:14:33.720 --> 00:14:37.170
But in Ukraine, all this
data is public and available

00:14:37.170 --> 00:14:40.530
on website of Ministry of
Social Policy of Ukraine;

00:14:40.530 --> 00:14:43.110
however colleagues have
visualized this dynamic

00:14:43.110 --> 00:14:43.943
in very good way.

00:14:43.943 --> 00:14:48.180
We see that the number of
registered IDPS in Ukraine

00:14:48.180 --> 00:14:50.733
remains relatively stable.

00:14:52.204 --> 00:14:54.371
Just higher in allocation.

00:14:55.335 --> 00:14:59.340
Compared to 2015, the active combat phase,

00:14:59.340 --> 00:15:02.550
in the following years
we see a slight decrease

00:15:02.550 --> 00:15:05.064
in the number of registered IDPs,

00:15:05.064 --> 00:15:08.100
and there are various
reasons behind these decrease

00:15:08.100 --> 00:15:09.360
in number of the IDP.

00:15:09.360 --> 00:15:11.370
First of all is natural
deaths of many people

00:15:11.370 --> 00:15:15.480
because, many people of retirement age,

00:15:15.480 --> 00:15:16.773
elderly people who,

00:15:18.060 --> 00:15:20.864
push to flow because of the war.

00:15:20.864 --> 00:15:23.400
Then it's purchase of private flats,

00:15:23.400 --> 00:15:26.070
in Ukraine, it's very important
moment, in Ukraine people,

00:15:26.070 --> 00:15:28.770
in this case, get a new registration

00:15:28.770 --> 00:15:31.143
and lose their IDP status.

00:15:31.980 --> 00:15:35.280
Then, it's fictitious
IDP have been removed

00:15:35.280 --> 00:15:39.690
from the database after
series of state checks,

00:15:39.690 --> 00:15:42.461
which removed some part of the IDPs

00:15:42.461 --> 00:15:45.000
and some IDPs continued their displacement

00:15:45.000 --> 00:15:47.160
and left for other countries,

00:15:47.160 --> 00:15:49.769
and some people have
returned to the occupied

00:15:49.769 --> 00:15:52.980
territories because of financial
difficulties in Ukraine,

00:15:52.980 --> 00:15:55.623
mainly due to problems with renting

00:15:55.623 --> 00:15:58.440
accommodation and employment.

00:15:58.440 --> 00:16:03.330
It's a very crucial
combination for many IDPs

00:16:03.330 --> 00:16:06.990
because, for renting the
house you need to have a work.

00:16:06.990 --> 00:16:10.320
If you haven't work you'll have
not money for renting houses

00:16:10.320 --> 00:16:11.970
and this combination was very important

00:16:11.970 --> 00:16:14.250
for many people when they decide

00:16:14.250 --> 00:16:17.310
to return to the occupied territory.

00:16:17.310 --> 00:16:19.590
And there is another important
point for understanding

00:16:19.590 --> 00:16:22.170
the scale of internal displacement.

00:16:22.170 --> 00:16:25.857
During our first interview in 2014-2015,

00:16:27.183 --> 00:16:31.200
we identified that up to
one-third IDP are avoiding

00:16:31.200 --> 00:16:33.990
official registration for various reasons.

00:16:33.990 --> 00:16:37.050
These practices was more pronounced among

00:16:37.050 --> 00:16:40.260
young IDPs, who described their motives

00:16:40.260 --> 00:16:44.250
for avoiding registration
as both reluctance

00:16:44.250 --> 00:16:47.490
to pass on their problems to the state,

00:16:47.490 --> 00:16:50.760
and a desire to avoid stigmatization.

00:16:50.760 --> 00:16:53.699
And among the wealthier part of IDPs,

00:16:53.699 --> 00:16:56.690
who were able to quickly purchase housing

00:16:56.690 --> 00:16:58.440
in the controlled part of Ukraine

00:16:58.440 --> 00:17:00.243
and change their registration.

00:17:04.260 --> 00:17:08.100
On this slide, on the map to the left,

00:17:08.100 --> 00:17:09.660
yeah.

00:17:09.660 --> 00:17:14.660
You can see the location of
officially registered IDPs

00:17:14.970 --> 00:17:18.330
before the full-scale Russian aggression.

00:17:18.330 --> 00:17:21.570
As you can see, most
of the displaced person

00:17:21.570 --> 00:17:25.290
tried to settle close to their homes,

00:17:25.290 --> 00:17:27.177
in the occupied territory.

00:17:27.177 --> 00:17:29.820
Donetsk and Luhansk oblast were leaders

00:17:29.820 --> 00:17:32.250
in the number of IDPs.

00:17:32.250 --> 00:17:33.727
The large number of IDPs in Kyiv

00:17:33.727 --> 00:17:38.610
was due to Kyiv's attractiveness
for IDPs as a capital,

00:17:38.610 --> 00:17:41.880
where all the key state
structures are located.

00:17:41.880 --> 00:17:45.090
The choice in favor of
Kyiv was rationally based

00:17:45.090 --> 00:17:47.640
on the fact that it put IDPs close

00:17:47.640 --> 00:17:51.030
to the governmental structures
providing assistance.

00:17:51.030 --> 00:17:54.540
This shortened the distance
and accelerated the process

00:17:54.540 --> 00:17:56.190
of solving many problems.

00:17:56.190 --> 00:17:59.820
And then you see another line of regions,

00:17:59.820 --> 00:18:03.390
here on this map, it's
Kharkiv, Dnipro, and Zaporozhye

00:18:03.390 --> 00:18:04.700
which are also located

00:18:04.700 --> 00:18:07.530
in the eastern and
south-eastern part of Ukraine.

00:18:07.530 --> 00:18:10.710
In this case, people who
have been been affected

00:18:10.710 --> 00:18:12.898
by the war could resettle in territories

00:18:12.898 --> 00:18:16.620
that remained under the control
of the Ukrainian government,

00:18:16.620 --> 00:18:21.140
and as close as possible
to where they used to live.

00:18:21.140 --> 00:18:23.400
At the moment very important
for many of these people

00:18:23.400 --> 00:18:27.699
is mostly Russian speaking regions.

00:18:27.699 --> 00:18:30.600
On the right-hand side of the
slide you see a visualization

00:18:30.600 --> 00:18:33.530
of displacement taking place in the face

00:18:33.530 --> 00:18:36.300
of full-scale Russian aggression in 2022.

00:18:37.200 --> 00:18:39.810
The situation is radically different.

00:18:39.810 --> 00:18:43.650
Most of the IDPs have
tried to move as far away

00:18:43.650 --> 00:18:45.960
from the armed conflict zone as possible.

00:18:45.960 --> 00:18:48.656
See this difference between
these two situation,

00:18:48.656 --> 00:18:49.983
between these two maps.

00:18:52.290 --> 00:18:54.330
Another interesting detail become apparent

00:18:54.330 --> 00:18:57.540
if we look at the settlement map of IDPs

00:18:57.540 --> 00:19:00.990
in terms of their
socio-demographic parameters.

00:19:00.990 --> 00:19:05.340
In 2015, the United Nation
High Commission for Refugees

00:19:05.340 --> 00:19:08.803
published a map, showing not
only the total number of IDPs

00:19:08.803 --> 00:19:12.420
by oblast, but also the
data on the proportion

00:19:12.420 --> 00:19:14.820
of different groups of forced migrants

00:19:14.820 --> 00:19:18.150
such as pensioners, working age adults,

00:19:18.150 --> 00:19:19.533
children, and disabled.

00:19:20.837 --> 00:19:22.980
Here, too, we see an interesting pattern.

00:19:22.980 --> 00:19:25.530
Among those registered
as close as possible

00:19:25.530 --> 00:19:29.043
to the dividing line, pensioners
are the most numerous.

00:19:29.880 --> 00:19:33.150
This is another manifestation
of the problem of counting

00:19:33.150 --> 00:19:36.420
the real number of IDPs
in Ukraine after starting

00:19:36.420 --> 00:19:39.210
the first wave of Russian
aggression in 2014.

00:19:39.210 --> 00:19:43.350
A significant number of
pensioners registered close

00:19:43.350 --> 00:19:47.180
to the demarcation line
spent most of their time

00:19:47.180 --> 00:19:50.340
in their homes in the occupied territory,

00:19:50.340 --> 00:19:52.800
due to financial difficulties

00:19:52.800 --> 00:19:57.800
and the absence of a systematic
policy of state support.

00:19:58.350 --> 00:20:02.160
They came to Ukraine to undergo
identification procedure,

00:20:02.160 --> 00:20:06.780
which remained compulsory
and regular every few months,

00:20:06.780 --> 00:20:09.243
for pensioner before the pandemic.

00:20:12.687 --> 00:20:14.790
The situation changed dramatically

00:20:14.790 --> 00:20:19.170
with the onset of the COVID-19 pandemic.

00:20:19.170 --> 00:20:21.780
While before the
pandemic, there were about

00:20:21.780 --> 00:20:25.170
a million monthly crossing
in both direction,

00:20:25.170 --> 00:20:26.820
very good sources,

00:20:26.820 --> 00:20:29.310
where, it's a good app which show you,

00:20:29.310 --> 00:20:32.253
in the dynamic of this data.

00:20:34.336 --> 00:20:36.187
While before the
pandemic, there were about

00:20:36.187 --> 00:20:39.084
a million monthly crossing
in both directions,

00:20:39.084 --> 00:20:42.750
with the April 2020 restrictions
on crossing at checkpoints,

00:20:42.750 --> 00:20:46.140
the number of monthly
crossings fell to 2,000

00:20:46.140 --> 00:20:49.170
and was no longer back
to the previous level

00:20:49.170 --> 00:20:51.333
until the full-scale Russian invasion.

00:20:52.483 --> 00:20:54.300
And, these facts demonstrate

00:20:54.300 --> 00:20:57.510
the problematic nature
of counting the actual

00:20:57.510 --> 00:21:01.560
number of IDPs, as
registration does not always

00:21:01.560 --> 00:21:04.920
indicate a person's actual presence

00:21:04.920 --> 00:21:07.923
in the territory controlled
by Ukrainian government.

00:21:10.650 --> 00:21:12.540
And on this slide,

00:21:12.540 --> 00:21:16.530
you see the main vectors of
external forced migration

00:21:16.530 --> 00:21:20.310
from Ukraine after the start
of Russian aggression in 2014.

00:21:20.310 --> 00:21:22.923
The key problem with
external migration was,

00:21:22.923 --> 00:21:27.923
that in most cases, Ukrainians
left as labor migrants.

00:21:28.170 --> 00:21:29.310
The countries of arrival

00:21:29.310 --> 00:21:33.180
did not grant refugee
status in most cases,

00:21:33.180 --> 00:21:35.490
as a small part of the
territory of Ukraine

00:21:35.490 --> 00:21:38.670
was covered by the war,
it was the main argument.

00:21:38.670 --> 00:21:42.630
And Ukrainians themselves
have not sought refugee status

00:21:42.630 --> 00:21:45.423
because of significant
restrictions on employment.

00:21:47.430 --> 00:21:49.720
And now I, want to share

00:21:53.470 --> 00:21:58.260
some of our ideas with which
found during our interviews,

00:21:58.260 --> 00:21:59.400
in depth interviews with people.

00:21:59.400 --> 00:22:02.400
But before I start
talking about some groups

00:22:02.400 --> 00:22:04.740
of displaced population,

00:22:04.740 --> 00:22:06.930
I wanted to come back
to this whole picture

00:22:06.930 --> 00:22:09.349
which give us opportunity
with what happened

00:22:09.349 --> 00:22:11.280
in Ukraine on this stage.

00:22:11.280 --> 00:22:13.080
In World Migration report, 2020,

00:22:13.080 --> 00:22:15.870
Ukraine mentioned in the
list of top 20 countries

00:22:15.870 --> 00:22:18.900
with the largest stock of
internally displaced persons

00:22:18.900 --> 00:22:22.353
by conflict and violence
at the end of 2018.

00:22:22.353 --> 00:22:25.170
With the beginning of
Russian aggression in 2014,

00:22:25.170 --> 00:22:27.270
more than 2 million people have left

00:22:27.270 --> 00:22:28.530
the uncontrolled
territories of the Ukraine,

00:22:28.530 --> 00:22:31.470
and were forced to move both
to other parts of Ukraine

00:22:31.470 --> 00:22:33.090
and beyond its borders.

00:22:33.090 --> 00:22:35.100
According to the Ministry
of Social Policy,

00:22:35.100 --> 00:22:39.450
after 2015 and before the
full-scale Russian invasion,

00:22:39.450 --> 00:22:42.090
the number of registered
internally displaced person

00:22:42.090 --> 00:22:44.290
was relatively stable
at around 1.5 million.

00:22:46.475 --> 00:22:47.913
Residents of war-torn territories

00:22:47.913 --> 00:22:50.520
have also been fleeing the country,

00:22:50.520 --> 00:22:54.840
or other countries since 2014.

00:22:54.840 --> 00:22:56.357
As of December 2015,

00:22:56.357 --> 00:22:59.370
in the countries that have
common borders with Ukraine,

00:22:59.370 --> 00:23:02.460
such as Russia, Belarus, Poland,
Slovakia, Hungary, Moldova,

00:23:02.460 --> 00:23:03.717
there were more than,

00:23:05.853 --> 00:23:09.923
it's near 400,000
Ukrainians seeking refuges,

00:23:09.923 --> 00:23:14.910
and more than 700,000
Ukrainians seeking other forms

00:23:14.910 --> 00:23:17.767
of legal stay in the mentioned countries.

00:23:17.767 --> 00:23:20.925
The estimated population
of the occupied territories

00:23:20.925 --> 00:23:24.040
of Donetsk and Luhansk
regions is 3.7 million.

00:23:24.040 --> 00:23:25.380
These are mostly people

00:23:25.380 --> 00:23:28.951
who formally remain the
citizens of Ukraine as a group.

00:23:28.951 --> 00:23:30.780
It's very important group of information

00:23:30.780 --> 00:23:35.130
which have not enough
attention in many researchers

00:23:35.130 --> 00:23:37.383
and in many policies, state policies.

00:23:38.250 --> 00:23:42.750
Since 2014, in all our
research we have focused

00:23:42.750 --> 00:23:46.800
on different groups of
people affected by the war.

00:23:46.800 --> 00:23:49.740
These include, first of all,
internally displaced persons

00:23:49.740 --> 00:23:54.690
with their own strategies
of migration and adaptation;

00:23:54.690 --> 00:23:59.038
external migrants who were
forced to move by the war;

00:23:59.038 --> 00:24:01.740
people residing in the
occupied territories,

00:24:01.740 --> 00:24:05.520
who are witnessing a transformation
of their everyday lives;

00:24:05.520 --> 00:24:07.320
and residents of so-called gray zone,

00:24:07.320 --> 00:24:09.390
the territory between
the two conflict parties,

00:24:09.390 --> 00:24:11.160
beyond their jurisdictions,

00:24:11.160 --> 00:24:15.180
and therefore, where no one
was responsible for the lives

00:24:15.180 --> 00:24:18.214
and wellbeing of people
who lived here today.

00:24:18.214 --> 00:24:20.850
In all these cases, the
focus was on the people

00:24:20.850 --> 00:24:23.370
whose life-worlds have been ruined,

00:24:23.370 --> 00:24:25.140
and who have found themselves

00:24:25.140 --> 00:24:28.020
in extreme circumstances of war,

00:24:28.020 --> 00:24:30.810
forced migration, and/or under occupation.

00:24:30.810 --> 00:24:34.037
Together, all of these
studies could be referred to

00:24:34.037 --> 00:24:38.970
as "the average person's life
under extreme circumstances".

00:24:38.970 --> 00:24:41.143
In the context of all this our research,

00:24:41.143 --> 00:24:43.740
a range of social phenomena arise,

00:24:43.740 --> 00:24:45.810
which deserve careful attention

00:24:45.810 --> 00:24:47.817
since they concern the recognition

00:24:47.817 --> 00:24:51.810
of people's rights to
full life and protection.

00:24:51.810 --> 00:24:54.330
These social phenomena
include stigmatization,

00:24:54.330 --> 00:24:56.670
social exclusion, social deprivation,

00:24:56.670 --> 00:25:00.483
discrimination and practices of othering.

00:25:02.130 --> 00:25:05.803
People residing in the
territories engulfed by the war

00:25:05.803 --> 00:25:10.290
in 2014 received no
support from the government

00:25:10.290 --> 00:25:12.690
at the beginning of the military conflict.

00:25:12.690 --> 00:25:15.870
A very small number of
people were evacuated,

00:25:15.870 --> 00:25:18.240
mostly by volunteer groups.

00:25:18.240 --> 00:25:20.287
Another important point in the perception

00:25:20.287 --> 00:25:22.820
of forced displacement was that IDPs,

00:25:22.820 --> 00:25:25.347
in the context of Ukrainian state policy,

00:25:25.347 --> 00:25:29.193
were and are perceived
as a homogeneous group.

00:25:30.690 --> 00:25:34.213
However, this vision of IDPs
does not respond to the needs,

00:25:34.213 --> 00:25:37.497
motivations and behaviors of
different segments of IDPs,

00:25:37.497 --> 00:25:40.140
and we try to segment this group,

00:25:40.140 --> 00:25:43.766
and look at different
segments of this big group

00:25:43.766 --> 00:25:46.410
which are absolutely not homogenous.

00:25:46.410 --> 00:25:49.680
In all reports on the
situation of IDPs in Ukraine,

00:25:49.680 --> 00:25:53.670
the group of people of retirement
age, that's one segment,

00:25:53.670 --> 00:25:56.190
receives an enormous amount of attention,

00:25:56.190 --> 00:25:57.120
that's absolutely right.

00:25:57.120 --> 00:26:02.010
The payment of pensions has
been and remains problematic.

00:26:02.010 --> 00:26:06.330
In the solidarity model that
exists in Ukraine for today,

00:26:06.330 --> 00:26:09.240
pensions should be linked to citizenship,

00:26:09.240 --> 00:26:12.450
not to local budgets
and social assistance.

00:26:12.450 --> 00:26:15.947
But the problem was that pensions in 2014

00:26:16.942 --> 00:26:19.053
were tied to local budgets.

00:26:19.890 --> 00:26:22.192
A very big problem in
receiving the pension

00:26:22.192 --> 00:26:23.763
in this situation.

00:26:23.763 --> 00:26:26.250
And because in the first
wave of displacement

00:26:26.250 --> 00:26:29.400
caused by Russian aggression in 2014,

00:26:29.400 --> 00:26:33.600
people of retirement age
faced the need to register

00:26:33.600 --> 00:26:36.300
in Ukrainian
government-controlled territory

00:26:36.300 --> 00:26:40.320
and undergo humiliating
identification procedures

00:26:40.320 --> 00:26:41.940
every few months.

00:26:41.940 --> 00:26:43.900
This has, on the one hand,

00:26:44.844 --> 00:26:47.963
inflated the real number of IDPs,

00:26:47.963 --> 00:26:51.839
forcing people to fictitiously register

00:26:51.839 --> 00:26:53.680
for receiving pensions.

00:26:53.680 --> 00:26:56.212
And on the other hand, it
has created a situation

00:26:56.212 --> 00:26:58.290
in which people of retirement age

00:26:58.290 --> 00:27:01.710
had to be identified in
Oshchadbank in Ukraine

00:27:01.710 --> 00:27:04.680
every several months,
had to stand in queues

00:27:04.680 --> 00:27:07.470
and risked their lives
undergoing humiliating

00:27:07.470 --> 00:27:11.700
and meaningless identification procedures

00:27:11.700 --> 00:27:15.180
in Ukrainian banks and state institutions.

00:27:15.180 --> 00:27:17.130
I want to show you some photos

00:27:17.130 --> 00:27:19.980
that I took during one of my studies,

00:27:19.980 --> 00:27:21.870
during my study trip.

00:27:21.870 --> 00:27:26.218
These are people who are
line up to cross the border

00:27:26.218 --> 00:27:27.717
between the Ukrainian-controlled

00:27:27.717 --> 00:27:30.330
and non-government-controlled
parts of Luhansk oblast.

00:27:30.330 --> 00:27:31.890
So called zero point.

00:27:31.890 --> 00:27:34.560
And you see here how it look like

00:27:34.560 --> 00:27:37.740
it's usually near 6 people in one line

00:27:37.740 --> 00:27:40.260
and very, very long queues.

00:27:40.260 --> 00:27:43.623
These people who are waiting
for this crossing the border.

00:27:44.739 --> 00:27:49.050
Moreover, it's a other
look on the same position,

00:27:49.050 --> 00:27:52.560
moreover, at this checkpoint,

00:27:52.560 --> 00:27:55.383
people had to cross,
unfortunately it's not my photo,

00:27:56.721 --> 00:27:58.690
the credit for this was mine

00:27:58.690 --> 00:28:01.710
but, this I use in open
sources to show you,

00:28:01.710 --> 00:28:04.140
how it was during long period.

00:28:04.140 --> 00:28:06.213
Now this broken, most are repaired.

00:28:07.140 --> 00:28:08.220
Moreover, at this checkpoint,

00:28:08.220 --> 00:28:10.680
people had to cross over
the destroyed bridge,

00:28:10.680 --> 00:28:13.383
as you can see on this slide.

00:28:16.470 --> 00:28:21.470
Try to imagine how it was
for people of retirement age

00:28:22.680 --> 00:28:24.870
or elderly people, it's
absolutely horrible situation

00:28:24.870 --> 00:28:25.953
for many of them.

00:28:27.658 --> 00:28:32.070
This policy of identification processes

00:28:32.070 --> 00:28:36.310
especially affected elderly
people with limited mobility

00:28:37.200 --> 00:28:39.780
that end up being completely denied

00:28:39.780 --> 00:28:41.700
access to pension provision.

00:28:41.700 --> 00:28:44.817
It has been estimated that
the number of such people

00:28:44.817 --> 00:28:49.680
unable to receive a pension in 2014-2015

00:28:49.680 --> 00:28:51.870
was over half a million.

00:28:51.870 --> 00:28:54.450
Those who dare to cross
the demarcation line

00:28:54.450 --> 00:28:58.590
for the identification
procedure suffered as well.

00:28:58.590 --> 00:29:02.250
Research participants estimate
that crossing the border line

00:29:02.250 --> 00:29:07.250
could take anywhere from
several hours to several days.

00:29:09.702 --> 00:29:12.510
It's really long queues,
it's really much time.

00:29:12.510 --> 00:29:16.570
You stay in these queues
and maybe several blocks

00:29:18.930 --> 00:29:22.920
something positive but,
it's clear positive moment,

00:29:22.920 --> 00:29:27.540
but we see how many businesses
arise around this situation.

00:29:27.540 --> 00:29:30.990
Many people who come to these
people staying in queues

00:29:30.990 --> 00:29:35.990
and propose, different
kind of help, to buy food,

00:29:36.030 --> 00:29:37.473
to staying in the line,

00:29:40.419 --> 00:29:44.130
to sell place in the queues,
et cetera, et cetera.

00:29:44.130 --> 00:29:46.350
So many businesses, very,
very interesting businesses,

00:29:46.350 --> 00:29:48.000
how it's organized here.

00:29:48.000 --> 00:29:50.700
But elderly people who died while passing

00:29:50.700 --> 00:29:51.930
through the demarcation line,

00:29:51.930 --> 00:29:55.343
contribute to the statistics
of civilian victims.

00:29:55.343 --> 00:29:57.860
The exact number is unknown

00:29:57.860 --> 00:30:00.300
as there is no systematic
calculation of people

00:30:00.300 --> 00:30:04.080
who died in queues on both
sides of the demarcation line.

00:30:04.080 --> 00:30:06.780
In the pandemic, the
checkpoints were closed

00:30:06.780 --> 00:30:08.550
and remained partially closed

00:30:08.550 --> 00:30:11.340
until the outbreak of the full-scale war.

00:30:11.340 --> 00:30:13.200
In this time the only one option

00:30:13.200 --> 00:30:14.537
was to enter through the territory

00:30:14.537 --> 00:30:16.590
of the Russian Federation,

00:30:16.590 --> 00:30:19.470
but in this case people must pay a fine

00:30:19.470 --> 00:30:22.020
for illegally crossing the border.

00:30:22.020 --> 00:30:23.880
In 2020, the Ukrainian government

00:30:23.880 --> 00:30:28.143
softened this measure and
no fines were imposed.

00:30:28.143 --> 00:30:29.910
With the full-scale Russian invasion,

00:30:29.910 --> 00:30:33.360
the scope of the occupied
territories has increased.

00:30:33.360 --> 00:30:36.570
Today it is no longer just a
problem of Ukrainian citizens

00:30:36.570 --> 00:30:39.360
living under occupation
on the Crimean peninsula,

00:30:39.360 --> 00:30:41.057
parts of Donetsk and Luhansk oblast.

00:30:41.057 --> 00:30:42.870
It is now also a problem for residents

00:30:42.870 --> 00:30:45.670
of parts of Kherson, Kharkiv
and Zaporizhzhia regions.

00:30:45.670 --> 00:30:49.620
While the procedure for accruing pensions

00:30:49.620 --> 00:30:53.160
has been simplified with a bank card,

00:30:53.160 --> 00:30:55.650
it is automatically extended

00:30:55.650 --> 00:30:58.527
and money is transferred to the account,

00:30:58.527 --> 00:31:01.800
the problem for pensioners
in the occupied territories

00:31:01.800 --> 00:31:06.240
remains the inability
to receive this money.

00:31:06.240 --> 00:31:07.710
The Russian Federation

00:31:07.710 --> 00:31:10.290
is blocking Ukrainian
mobile communications

00:31:10.290 --> 00:31:13.890
and restricting access
to Ukrainian websites

00:31:13.890 --> 00:31:15.750
and services via the Internet

00:31:15.750 --> 00:31:18.120
for residents of the occupied territories.

00:31:18.120 --> 00:31:21.960
Even if they have a bank card
with money transferred to it,

00:31:21.960 --> 00:31:24.807
there is no place to cash it

00:31:24.807 --> 00:31:28.230
and it is not possible
to pay with it by phone,

00:31:28.230 --> 00:31:29.790
in shops, et cetera.

00:31:29.790 --> 00:31:32.640
As a result, people
either have to find ways

00:31:32.640 --> 00:31:35.280
to travel to Ukrainian
government-controlled areas,

00:31:35.280 --> 00:31:38.460
which is difficult to do
given the hostilities,

00:31:38.460 --> 00:31:41.080
or turn to illegal money changers

00:31:42.510 --> 00:31:47.120
and lose a significant
percentage of their transfers.

00:31:47.120 --> 00:31:50.687
In parallel the banks ensure that there

00:31:50.687 --> 00:31:55.290
are no long interruptions
in the use of the card,

00:31:55.290 --> 00:31:57.030
at least six months,

00:31:57.030 --> 00:32:00.461
as this could indicate the possible death

00:32:00.461 --> 00:32:03.540
of the beneficiary of the pension.

00:32:03.540 --> 00:32:06.450
In such a case, payments are suspended

00:32:06.450 --> 00:32:09.663
until the circumstances are clarified.

00:32:10.650 --> 00:32:12.840
However, for the real elderly person

00:32:12.840 --> 00:32:15.480
the bureaucratic formula
of "clarification"

00:32:15.480 --> 00:32:18.660
means finding difficult ways to travel

00:32:18.660 --> 00:32:21.913
to the controlled territory
at the risk of death.

00:32:26.279 --> 00:32:31.200
As of today, after heated
discussion about the possibility

00:32:31.200 --> 00:32:34.140
of paying pensions to so
called "collaborators"

00:32:34.140 --> 00:32:36.570
seem to have subsided.

00:32:36.570 --> 00:32:40.090
The Ukrainian state
demonstrates maximum loyalty

00:32:40.090 --> 00:32:45.003
in the accrual of pensions
to those who have earned it

00:32:46.110 --> 00:32:49.350
according to the solidarity
pension system in Ukraine,

00:32:49.350 --> 00:32:52.432
wherever these people are located.

00:32:52.432 --> 00:32:56.460
After earlier ambiguous
statements about the population

00:32:56.460 --> 00:32:58.110
of the occupied territories,

00:32:58.110 --> 00:33:00.600
government officials
now clearly articulate

00:33:00.600 --> 00:33:04.140
the people as the state's primary value.

00:33:04.140 --> 00:33:06.819
However, the reality of war

00:33:06.819 --> 00:33:10.201
and the lack of effective
mechanisms for receiving payments

00:33:10.201 --> 00:33:13.687
where all options are
blocked by the aggressor

00:33:13.687 --> 00:33:15.480
remain an problem.

00:33:15.480 --> 00:33:17.430
Its importance lies in the fact that

00:33:17.430 --> 00:33:19.789
for people in the occupied territories

00:33:19.789 --> 00:33:23.490
it is often a question of survival,

00:33:23.490 --> 00:33:26.340
which affects how they
evaluate the ability

00:33:26.340 --> 00:33:29.430
of their own state to protect them.

00:33:29.430 --> 00:33:32.460
Why am I highlighting this difference

00:33:32.460 --> 00:33:34.410
in situation and policy

00:33:34.410 --> 00:33:37.890
in the first and second
stages of Russian aggression?

00:33:37.890 --> 00:33:40.410
Because the current policy
of the Ukrainian state

00:33:40.410 --> 00:33:41.520
and society towards the victims

00:33:41.520 --> 00:33:44.220
of the first wave of Russian aggression,

00:33:44.220 --> 00:33:49.220
considerably more humane and
less discriminatory in nature,

00:33:49.320 --> 00:33:51.780
was the result of the struggle

00:33:51.780 --> 00:33:54.393
for their rights by the first IDPs,

00:33:55.625 --> 00:34:00.625
and these complicated public
debates in the country.

00:34:01.476 --> 00:34:04.553
But back to the situation in 2014.

00:34:04.553 --> 00:34:07.980
Another group seriously
affected by the conflict

00:34:07.980 --> 00:34:10.770
was people of pre-retirement age.

00:34:10.770 --> 00:34:12.833
People from this category
had little chance

00:34:12.833 --> 00:34:15.060
of employment in Ukraine.

00:34:15.060 --> 00:34:19.170
Have this very big problem
of age discrimination

00:34:19.170 --> 00:34:22.770
and therefore they find themselves

00:34:22.770 --> 00:34:25.620
in an extremely tough situation

00:34:25.620 --> 00:34:27.900
as a result of forced displacement.

00:34:27.900 --> 00:34:32.593
The amount of state aid
for incapacitated persons

00:34:33.990 --> 00:34:37.170
was 1000 UAH, it's near $25 monthly,

00:34:41.880 --> 00:34:46.880
442 UAH, it's near $20 to $30,
for your comparison, monthly

00:34:48.000 --> 00:34:49.713
for those who are able to work.

00:34:51.402 --> 00:34:54.240
It's very small sum, it's
very, very small amount.

00:34:54.240 --> 00:34:58.620
Such payments could not even
cover basic food expenses,

00:34:58.620 --> 00:35:01.320
and were, of course, not
enough to rent housing.

00:35:01.320 --> 00:35:03.183
Moreover, this category of people

00:35:03.183 --> 00:35:05.220
have been completely invisible

00:35:05.220 --> 00:35:07.590
for most of the
international organizations

00:35:07.590 --> 00:35:09.840
that help people affected by war.

00:35:09.840 --> 00:35:12.000
International organizations mostly worked

00:35:12.000 --> 00:35:13.053
with target groups,

00:35:14.291 --> 00:35:16.890
on this stage, such as retired people,

00:35:16.890 --> 00:35:18.180
people with disabilities,

00:35:18.180 --> 00:35:20.820
mothers with little
children, or large family.

00:35:20.820 --> 00:35:23.550
But this group of people,

00:35:23.550 --> 00:35:25.680
have absolutely lack of help.

00:35:27.447 --> 00:35:29.250
And this group remained ignored

00:35:29.250 --> 00:35:30.930
by international organizations,

00:35:30.930 --> 00:35:35.930
also this category was without
help and stay invisible.

00:35:37.470 --> 00:35:42.470
Then, under these
conditions of divided worlds

00:35:42.600 --> 00:35:46.897
and separated families,
there are 'unmourned' deaths

00:35:47.770 --> 00:35:50.850
that are rarely described
in academic literature.

00:35:50.850 --> 00:35:52.800
Before the full-scale Russian invasion,

00:35:52.800 --> 00:35:55.290
the average person to enter
the occupied territory

00:35:55.290 --> 00:35:58.517
should send a request to the
Security Service of Ukraine

00:35:58.517 --> 00:35:59.923
and to receive, within 10 days,

00:35:59.923 --> 00:36:02.490
a permit for border crossing.

00:36:02.490 --> 00:36:06.220
Important life events, the
death or illness of a relative,

00:36:06.220 --> 00:36:10.620
a family reunion, could
accelerate the procedure,

00:36:10.620 --> 00:36:12.750
although one still has to spend time

00:36:12.750 --> 00:36:15.900
providing official
confirmation of life events.

00:36:15.900 --> 00:36:19.530
Also, a decision to let
someone cross the line

00:36:19.530 --> 00:36:24.530
was made arbitrarily by
a certain military person

00:36:24.560 --> 00:36:28.740
at the checkpoint, on any
side of the demarcation line.

00:36:28.740 --> 00:36:31.020
The pandemic have worsened this problem:

00:36:31.020 --> 00:36:35.310
people were being denied their
right to cross the border

00:36:35.310 --> 00:36:37.980
even when they could confirm purposes

00:36:37.980 --> 00:36:39.810
of important life events.

00:36:39.810 --> 00:36:44.070
The psychological traumas
of the deaths of loved ones

00:36:44.070 --> 00:36:46.260
who are separated by long distances

00:36:46.260 --> 00:36:50.220
and unmourned deaths are hard to assess.

00:36:50.220 --> 00:36:52.190
The interviews I conducted show that

00:36:52.190 --> 00:36:54.600
in the occupied territory, neighbors

00:36:54.600 --> 00:36:58.320
got together to bury elderly
people who lived alone,

00:36:58.320 --> 00:37:01.507
and their children never
saw their parents dead.

00:37:03.477 --> 00:37:05.370
Ordinary people who have found themselves

00:37:05.370 --> 00:37:06.750
in extreme circumstances

00:37:06.750 --> 00:37:10.190
somehow try to restore the
normality of their lives.

00:37:10.190 --> 00:37:13.470
At the same time, they
face certain barriers

00:37:13.470 --> 00:37:15.990
both at the level of
reactions in the society

00:37:15.990 --> 00:37:19.170
and at the institutional level.

00:37:19.170 --> 00:37:23.043
Internally displaced persons
were limited in their rights.

00:37:23.043 --> 00:37:26.820
All Ukrainian IDPs had been
deprived of the right to vote

00:37:26.820 --> 00:37:29.815
on local government elections until 2020.

00:37:29.815 --> 00:37:32.630
IDPs from Crimea are still
defined as non-residents

00:37:32.630 --> 00:37:34.320
by the country's bank system,

00:37:34.320 --> 00:37:36.930
and have limited access
to banking services.

00:37:36.930 --> 00:37:39.060
Prior to the full-scale aggression,

00:37:39.060 --> 00:37:43.560
those IDPs who were expecting
to receive state assistance

00:37:43.560 --> 00:37:45.900
had to undergo additional
humiliating procedures

00:37:45.900 --> 00:37:50.610
of identification and provide
detailed personal information

00:37:50.610 --> 00:37:53.520
including answering
questions about their loyalty

00:37:53.520 --> 00:37:55.110
to the Ukrainian state.

00:37:55.110 --> 00:37:56.460
I signed such document,

00:37:56.460 --> 00:37:58.923
I very well remember how it was.

00:37:59.790 --> 00:38:01.770
Which ultimately creates a feeling

00:38:01.770 --> 00:38:05.340
of their inferior citizenship.

00:38:05.340 --> 00:38:07.680
The awareness of all these problems

00:38:07.680 --> 00:38:10.860
and the active presence
of IDPs in public debates

00:38:10.860 --> 00:38:15.540
gave rise to many new
processes in Ukrainian society.

00:38:15.540 --> 00:38:19.290
For example, the demand
for medical services

00:38:19.290 --> 00:38:21.450
from more than a million people

00:38:21.450 --> 00:38:25.530
has simultaneously led to an accelerated

00:38:25.530 --> 00:38:27.990
reform of Ukraine's medical system.

00:38:27.990 --> 00:38:30.360
Today's medical system is no longer tied

00:38:30.360 --> 00:38:33.330
to the place of registration
of Ukrainian citizens

00:38:33.330 --> 00:38:35.220
and medical services are available

00:38:35.220 --> 00:38:36.990
everywhere in the country.

00:38:36.990 --> 00:38:39.090
The struggle for the political rights

00:38:39.090 --> 00:38:43.080
of IDPs has led to the
restoration of the right to vote

00:38:43.080 --> 00:38:46.260
not only in presidential
and parliamentary elections

00:38:46.260 --> 00:38:49.324
but also in local elections in 2020.

00:38:49.324 --> 00:38:51.900
In the Ukrainian context of
war and forced migration,

00:38:51.900 --> 00:38:56.040
we see many hybrid volunteering
practices and strategies,

00:38:56.040 --> 00:38:59.550
some of which grow into stable
and formalized activities

00:38:59.550 --> 00:39:01.980
and some of which fade away,

00:39:01.980 --> 00:39:04.080
both because of mitigation

00:39:04.080 --> 00:39:07.230
and because of burnout of many people

00:39:07.230 --> 00:39:10.452
who very actively involved in
this volunteering activity.

00:39:10.452 --> 00:39:11.924
We have this person,

00:39:11.924 --> 00:39:12.957
one had to have a very active person

00:39:12.957 --> 00:39:16.140
who do everything now in Ukrainian society

00:39:16.140 --> 00:39:18.690
but many of them faces this problem.

00:39:18.690 --> 00:39:21.420
However, both organized and spontaneous

00:39:21.420 --> 00:39:23.177
individual volunteering activities

00:39:23.177 --> 00:39:26.103
have a significant impact on society.

00:39:27.090 --> 00:39:30.930
For example, the volunteer psychologists

00:39:30.930 --> 00:39:32.940
who actively helped people

00:39:32.940 --> 00:39:36.480
to overcome stress on the Maidan in 2014

00:39:36.480 --> 00:39:39.540
have seen a change in people's attitudes

00:39:39.540 --> 00:39:41.490
towards their own mental health

00:39:41.490 --> 00:39:44.430
and towards professionals in this field.

00:39:44.430 --> 00:39:45.553
And we really now have,

00:39:45.553 --> 00:39:48.000
a very big change in Ukrainian society

00:39:48.000 --> 00:39:51.780
when people start looking
at their mental health,

00:39:51.780 --> 00:39:55.146
start thinking about what to
do with their mental health,

00:39:55.146 --> 00:39:58.200
how to change our systematical system

00:39:58.200 --> 00:40:01.560
and, given access to different services,

00:40:01.560 --> 00:40:03.780
which are connected to this new concept

00:40:03.780 --> 00:40:05.310
of mental health in Ukraine.

00:40:05.310 --> 00:40:08.760
It's really very important
change in society.

00:40:08.760 --> 00:40:10.956
For example in one of
our research we have,

00:40:10.956 --> 00:40:13.680
most part it was the
quantitative and qualitative part

00:40:13.680 --> 00:40:17.430
of this research about
different kind of practices

00:40:17.430 --> 00:40:19.320
connected to the mental health,

00:40:19.320 --> 00:40:22.290
and we ask our participants

00:40:22.290 --> 00:40:26.910
about how they try to solve their problem

00:40:26.910 --> 00:40:29.850
if they feel something
was wrong, what they do,

00:40:29.850 --> 00:40:34.590
and only 2.8% of our respondents says

00:40:34.590 --> 00:40:36.690
that they come to the professionals

00:40:36.690 --> 00:40:41.670
and ask about help in
mental health issues.

00:40:41.670 --> 00:40:42.660
It's very low level,

00:40:42.660 --> 00:40:44.830
it's really very low level

00:40:47.893 --> 00:40:51.350
of working under this issue
but, on the other hand,

00:40:51.350 --> 00:40:56.100
we see that these situation
accelerate it's development

00:40:56.100 --> 00:40:57.750
and people start thinking about this.

00:40:57.750 --> 00:40:59.940
It starts during the Maidan era.

00:40:59.940 --> 00:41:03.150
It was very important
that people see that,

00:41:03.150 --> 00:41:06.090
it's very difficult for them
to perceive the situation,

00:41:06.090 --> 00:41:07.497
to live in this situation.

00:41:07.497 --> 00:41:12.210
And many psychologist come to
the Maidan like volunteers,

00:41:12.210 --> 00:41:16.149
and start give this help to people

00:41:16.149 --> 00:41:21.149
and this accelerate this change

00:41:21.780 --> 00:41:24.608
in the culture of mental health,

00:41:24.608 --> 00:41:27.033
working on the mental skills issue.

00:41:28.410 --> 00:41:29.243
Then...

00:41:34.680 --> 00:41:36.843
Let me say several words about this.

00:41:37.762 --> 00:41:40.174
So I have to find the (indistinct).

00:41:40.174 --> 00:41:42.030
Several words about
volunteering practices,

00:41:42.030 --> 00:41:43.696
because I think it's very important.

00:41:43.696 --> 00:41:46.193
It's a very important
change in Ukrainian society.

00:41:47.700 --> 00:41:50.736
It's led us to present situation.

00:41:50.736 --> 00:41:53.730
Euromaidan was a pivotal moment

00:41:53.730 --> 00:41:56.910
that strengthened
solidarity among Ukrainians.

00:41:56.910 --> 00:41:59.220
While some third sector
groups demonstrated

00:41:59.220 --> 00:42:01.760
a transfer of social and human capital

00:42:01.760 --> 00:42:04.230
to the situation of mass displacement.

00:42:04.230 --> 00:42:06.780
I talk about volunteer,
first of all, in this field,

00:42:06.780 --> 00:42:10.230
of helping displaced persons.

00:42:10.230 --> 00:42:14.550
Many organizations active today
and providing help to IDPs

00:42:14.550 --> 00:42:18.120
were created at the very
beginning of the conflict

00:42:18.120 --> 00:42:20.250
based on spontaneous help.

00:42:20.250 --> 00:42:22.710
Many IDPs were involved
with these processes,

00:42:22.710 --> 00:42:25.650
many IDPs create these volunteering groups

00:42:25.650 --> 00:42:28.303
which are help to IDPs in this situation.

00:42:28.303 --> 00:42:33.098
For instance, the NGO Donbas
SOS was founded in 2014

00:42:33.098 --> 00:42:36.810
as a hotline used for the
quick coordination of help

00:42:36.810 --> 00:42:38.550
to the injured people.

00:42:38.550 --> 00:42:42.510
Today, the organization
unites 5 coordinators

00:42:42.510 --> 00:42:46.920
and around 40 volunteers of the hotline,

00:42:46.920 --> 00:42:49.560
also, there is a network
of regional volunteers

00:42:49.560 --> 00:42:54.060
in Donetsk, Lughansk, and
15 more regions of Ukraine.

00:42:54.060 --> 00:42:56.103
The other organization, Station Kharkiv,

00:42:57.360 --> 00:43:01.957
got its name because of the
specificity of its work,

00:43:01.957 --> 00:43:05.130
it was at the railway
station where the volunteers

00:43:05.130 --> 00:43:07.080
were meeting people, who ran
away from Donetsk and Lughansk

00:43:07.080 --> 00:43:11.202
regions affected by war, and
were helping with clothes,

00:43:11.202 --> 00:43:13.890
food, searching for accommodations,

00:43:13.890 --> 00:43:16.590
and providing legal
consultations for people

00:43:16.590 --> 00:43:18.720
forced to rapidly leave their homes

00:43:18.720 --> 00:43:22.350
for the indefinite time could have.

00:43:22.350 --> 00:43:25.560
Just in the period from
June till December 2014,

00:43:25.560 --> 00:43:29.400
Station Kharkiv managed to
find the places of residence

00:43:29.400 --> 00:43:33.030
for 30,000 of the displaced people.

00:43:33.030 --> 00:43:36.363
A survey of representatives of 138 NGOs,

00:43:38.160 --> 00:43:40.770
mainly in cooperation
with my colleague from

00:43:40.770 --> 00:43:43.050
Birmingham University, Irina Kuznetsova,

00:43:43.050 --> 00:43:45.600
and the previous research
about mental health,

00:43:45.600 --> 00:43:48.510
it's same situation,
we had a common project

00:43:48.510 --> 00:43:49.810
with Birmingham University

00:43:50.760 --> 00:43:53.103
and Ukrainian Catholic University.

00:43:54.070 --> 00:43:59.070
In this research we talk
with 138 representatives

00:43:59.540 --> 00:44:01.353
of NGOs in Ukraine.

00:44:03.090 --> 00:44:04.607
We see that...

00:44:09.660 --> 00:44:12.300
Many people who asked about help

00:44:12.300 --> 00:44:15.090
received the following
services, you can see here,

00:44:15.090 --> 00:44:18.240
it's consultations
regarding employment, 81%,

00:44:18.240 --> 00:44:21.217
psychological support, as you see this

00:44:21.217 --> 00:44:24.480
is a very big number of
people who receive this,

00:44:24.480 --> 00:44:29.480
but usually IDPs do not
ask about this help first.

00:44:30.343 --> 00:44:32.670
You need to propose the services

00:44:32.670 --> 00:44:36.660
if you want to involve
people in such communication.

00:44:36.660 --> 00:44:39.480
Then arrangement of the free time, 67%,

00:44:39.480 --> 00:44:42.930
assistance in the renewal
of payment of allowances

00:44:42.930 --> 00:44:46.860
and pensions and help with
finding the place of residence.

00:44:46.860 --> 00:44:49.200
In most cases NGOs and volunteers

00:44:49.200 --> 00:44:53.182
were forced to deal with the
multi-side case management:

00:44:53.182 --> 00:44:57.600
as a rule, people, who addressed for help,

00:44:57.600 --> 00:45:01.410
had a burden of the whole set of problems

00:45:01.410 --> 00:45:03.780
that couldn't be solved separately.

00:45:03.780 --> 00:45:07.620
For instance, the lost documents
caused the restrictions

00:45:07.620 --> 00:45:11.070
in the search for
accommodation and employment,

00:45:11.070 --> 00:45:14.670
and the non-payment of
pensions for many months,

00:45:14.670 --> 00:45:18.090
especially in the situation
of the single elderly people,

00:45:18.090 --> 00:45:22.491
causes the impossibility
to pay for the dwelling.

00:45:22.491 --> 00:45:24.390
It is important to note

00:45:24.390 --> 00:45:27.000
that the increase in mutual assistance

00:45:27.000 --> 00:45:29.873
is observed not only in
the South-East of Ukraine

00:45:29.873 --> 00:45:32.340
near the area of military actions

00:45:32.340 --> 00:45:34.767
but also in the other regions.

00:45:34.767 --> 00:45:37.480
The complex and flexible
work of volunteers involves

00:45:37.480 --> 00:45:40.947
good logistics and
management, builds on trust

00:45:40.947 --> 00:45:45.947
and informal connections, makes
use of sponsorship funding,

00:45:46.387 --> 00:45:48.663
and is publicly open.

00:45:50.580 --> 00:45:54.270
This widespread involvement of people

00:45:54.270 --> 00:45:58.960
in charitable activities
creates a feeling of engagement

00:45:58.960 --> 00:46:01.470
and solidarity that is crucial

00:46:01.470 --> 00:46:04.233
during the war and displacement.

00:46:05.091 --> 00:46:07.730
The need to cooperate
with business structures

00:46:07.730 --> 00:46:11.340
in one way or another
forms a complex interaction

00:46:11.340 --> 00:46:14.070
between them and flexible
volunteer structures,

00:46:14.070 --> 00:46:17.220
which often creates
profitable partnerships.

00:46:17.220 --> 00:46:21.296
Businesses, in most cases
these are small or medium-sized

00:46:21.296 --> 00:46:23.250
businesses, particularly
for this communication

00:46:23.250 --> 00:46:27.390
because large businesses usually
work with their charities,

00:46:27.390 --> 00:46:30.330
but small and middle businesses

00:46:30.330 --> 00:46:32.670
very actively communicate with volunteers.

00:46:32.670 --> 00:46:35.190
See volunteer groups as
professionals to whom

00:46:35.190 --> 00:46:38.970
they can entrust and delegate social work.

00:46:38.970 --> 00:46:42.600
And trust, as the basis
of these relationships,

00:46:42.600 --> 00:46:46.200
in turn, encourages
volunteers to worry about

00:46:46.200 --> 00:46:48.420
their reputational capital

00:46:48.420 --> 00:46:52.620
and strive to make their
activities as open as possible.

00:46:52.620 --> 00:46:55.020
It's very important processes.

00:46:55.020 --> 00:46:57.210
In a context of social
upheaval and awareness

00:46:57.210 --> 00:47:01.800
of the state's considerable
lag in its response,

00:47:01.800 --> 00:47:05.010
professionals who entered volunteering

00:47:05.010 --> 00:47:08.190
often saw it as an alternative

00:47:08.190 --> 00:47:12.270
to the unwieldy state machinery,

00:47:12.270 --> 00:47:14.700
as an opportunity to implement projects

00:47:14.700 --> 00:47:16.170
that they could not implement

00:47:16.170 --> 00:47:18.420
within the already existing structures.

00:47:18.420 --> 00:47:21.267
In this case, we can speak
of the powerful reformatory

00:47:21.267 --> 00:47:23.550
potential of the volunteer movement,

00:47:23.550 --> 00:47:27.000
which through its activity
shapes new models of behavior,

00:47:27.000 --> 00:47:30.273
and creates orientation
patterns, and values.

00:47:31.140 --> 00:47:34.620
Overall, it provides
a better understanding

00:47:34.620 --> 00:47:37.320
of the mechanism of
horizontal mobilization

00:47:37.320 --> 00:47:40.500
in Ukrainian society under
conditions of full-scale war

00:47:40.500 --> 00:47:43.500
and that unique situation of interaction

00:47:43.500 --> 00:47:45.540
between volunteer
groups, the third sector,

00:47:45.540 --> 00:47:47.613
business and state structures.

00:47:53.070 --> 00:47:55.710
As we can see, Ukrainian
society and representatives

00:47:55.710 --> 00:47:58.800
of state structures have
learnt from the situation

00:47:58.800 --> 00:48:02.190
in Ukrainian society as a
result of this first stage,

00:48:02.190 --> 00:48:04.440
of this first wave of Russian aggression.

00:48:04.440 --> 00:48:07.950
In most cases, these are
quite complex processes

00:48:07.950 --> 00:48:10.230
that deal with issues of identity,

00:48:10.230 --> 00:48:13.523
awareness of oneself
as a political nation,

00:48:13.523 --> 00:48:15.750
and the development of a new language

00:48:15.750 --> 00:48:19.920
for speaking about what
is going on around us.

00:48:19.920 --> 00:48:22.680
However, this situation
of Ukraine's visibility,

00:48:22.680 --> 00:48:26.310
of having a position that
sounds in different languages,

00:48:26.310 --> 00:48:29.400
is the result of the
discussions and experiences

00:48:29.400 --> 00:48:31.382
of this first eight-year period

00:48:31.382 --> 00:48:33.718
after the Russian aggression began.

00:48:33.718 --> 00:48:36.417
It was the experience of
volunteering during this period

00:48:36.417 --> 00:48:39.420
that allowed the volunteer
movement in Ukraine

00:48:39.420 --> 00:48:41.790
in the face of full-scale war

00:48:41.790 --> 00:48:45.570
to mobilize not in days, but in hours,

00:48:45.570 --> 00:48:47.880
and start working to defend the country

00:48:47.880 --> 00:48:50.403
and support the population and the army.

00:48:51.310 --> 00:48:53.490
The policies towards IDPs in Ukraine

00:48:53.490 --> 00:48:56.850
who fled as a result of the February 2022

00:48:56.850 --> 00:49:01.350
phase of the war use best
practices regarding IDPs

00:49:01.350 --> 00:49:03.681
that were formed during eight years

00:49:03.681 --> 00:49:07.640
and be aware of the areas
that were the most problematic

00:49:07.640 --> 00:49:11.950
regarding the response to the IDPs' needs.

00:49:11.950 --> 00:49:16.950
The areas where the response
was less efficient include:

00:49:17.160 --> 00:49:21.300
the lack of affordable and
accessible accommodation

00:49:21.300 --> 00:49:23.220
and the lack of social housing.

00:49:23.220 --> 00:49:24.650
We still have this problem in our country.

00:49:24.650 --> 00:49:28.189
Issues with pensions and benefits.

00:49:28.189 --> 00:49:30.690
The lack of recognition of the benefits

00:49:30.690 --> 00:49:32.520
of mental health support,

00:49:32.520 --> 00:49:34.560
stigmatization of mental health,

00:49:34.560 --> 00:49:38.430
and the lack of accessible
mental health support.

00:49:38.430 --> 00:49:42.333
Issues with the employment of
people of pre-retirement age.

00:49:43.590 --> 00:49:47.923
Given, that globally, the
number of IDPs increased

00:49:47.923 --> 00:49:52.622
from about 17 million in 1998

00:49:52.622 --> 00:49:56.730
to 59.1 million in 2021,

00:49:57.567 --> 00:49:59.280
and that many refugees

00:49:59.280 --> 00:50:02.190
experienced protracted
internal displacement

00:50:02.190 --> 00:50:04.530
before crossing international borders,

00:50:04.530 --> 00:50:08.100
the lessons from Ukraine can be valuable

00:50:08.100 --> 00:50:12.630
in addressing similar challenges
in different contexts.

00:50:12.630 --> 00:50:14.125
Thank you.

00:50:14.125 --> 00:50:16.875
(audience claps)

00:50:19.290 --> 00:50:20.310
- [Elena] My name is Elena.

00:50:20.310 --> 00:50:23.233
I teach foreign policy
at University of Avon.

00:50:23.233 --> 00:50:24.383
Thank you so much for being here

00:50:24.383 --> 00:50:26.580
and for all your work.

00:50:26.580 --> 00:50:28.833
I know it took quite a risk on your part.

00:50:30.330 --> 00:50:33.240
I was wondering if you knew
what proportion of IDPs

00:50:33.240 --> 00:50:35.190
have acquired multiple identifications,

00:50:35.190 --> 00:50:38.400
like Russian passports,
DNR or LNR passports.

00:50:38.400 --> 00:50:41.040
'Cause right now there's
a movement to terminate

00:50:41.040 --> 00:50:42.960
Ukrainian citizenship of individuals

00:50:42.960 --> 00:50:45.210
that are found to have
multiple identities,

00:50:45.210 --> 00:50:47.160
or multiple credentials.

00:50:47.160 --> 00:50:50.820
That way, in particular
the case with (indistinct)

00:50:50.820 --> 00:50:53.100
That was just recently disclosed.

00:50:53.100 --> 00:50:56.398
So there's a move in Ukraine
to quickly terminate,

00:50:56.398 --> 00:50:58.527
there's a constitutional process for that.

00:50:58.527 --> 00:51:02.007
But I was wondering if that
was part of your research

00:51:02.007 --> 00:51:06.120
and if you know,
proportionally, how many IDPs

00:51:06.120 --> 00:51:08.877
have actually managed to have
Russian passports and DNR

00:51:08.877 --> 00:51:11.790
and if it hurts them, right now,

00:51:11.790 --> 00:51:14.763
as they rebuild their lives?

00:51:15.660 --> 00:51:16.808
- Thank you very much for this question.

00:51:16.808 --> 00:51:18.330
It's really very important to understand

00:51:18.330 --> 00:51:21.420
what's going on in these
occupied territories.

00:51:21.420 --> 00:51:24.840
As you know the last data,
before the hostile aggression,

00:51:24.840 --> 00:51:29.840
it was 700,000 people who
take Russian passports.

00:51:30.750 --> 00:51:32.430
But unfortunately I don't know proportion

00:51:32.430 --> 00:51:36.794
because I usually use
interviews, not proportions.

00:51:36.794 --> 00:51:39.810
What I know about this situation,

00:51:39.810 --> 00:51:42.270
it's absolutely no
surprise for many people

00:51:42.270 --> 00:51:45.150
who are dealing with
this issue, like IDPs,

00:51:45.150 --> 00:51:47.910
they know, in the case of Crimea peninsula

00:51:47.910 --> 00:51:49.470
you need two passports.

00:51:49.470 --> 00:51:51.040
You need two passports because

00:51:52.204 --> 00:51:53.850
without these two passports

00:51:53.850 --> 00:51:57.177
you have not any opportunity
to go across the border.

00:51:57.177 --> 00:52:00.030
And the same situation
on occupied territory

00:52:00.030 --> 00:52:03.150
and what is little bit
problematic for the future

00:52:03.150 --> 00:52:05.760
from my point of view, the
people on this territory

00:52:05.760 --> 00:52:09.000
have an opportunity to
have five or six passports.

00:52:09.000 --> 00:52:10.572
How it's back in Ukrainian context,

00:52:10.572 --> 00:52:14.310
first of all, your national
Ukrainian passport,

00:52:14.310 --> 00:52:16.890
then we officially have
a right to have two

00:52:16.890 --> 00:52:19.024
international passports,
this is three passports.

00:52:19.024 --> 00:52:22.290
It's a passport of citizen
of Russian Federation,

00:52:22.290 --> 00:52:23.820
the international Russian passport

00:52:23.820 --> 00:52:27.180
and of course, passport
of citizen of DNR or LNR.

00:52:27.180 --> 00:52:29.730
It's five or six passports for one person,

00:52:29.730 --> 00:52:31.289
I think it's a little bit-

00:52:31.289 --> 00:52:33.557
- [Elena] Dual citizenship is
not allowed in the Ukraine.

00:52:33.557 --> 00:52:34.390
- Yeah?

00:52:34.390 --> 00:52:36.600
- [Elena] Dual citizenship is
not allowed in the Ukraine.

00:52:36.600 --> 00:52:39.810
You have to renounce one
of your citizenships.

00:52:39.810 --> 00:52:42.368
- Yes of course, but
still we are talking about

00:52:42.368 --> 00:52:45.368
real practice, not talking about
constitution and about law.

00:52:46.667 --> 00:52:47.580
It's real practice.

00:52:47.580 --> 00:52:49.409
They all have these six passports

00:52:49.409 --> 00:52:52.209
and in different contexts,
they use different passports.

00:52:53.280 --> 00:52:57.258
Why I think it's a very
important knowledge about this

00:52:57.258 --> 00:52:59.070
because it's a task for the future

00:52:59.070 --> 00:53:00.210
how to work with these people

00:53:00.210 --> 00:53:02.220
who have these different type of passport.

00:53:02.220 --> 00:53:04.626
But in this case we talk about, absolutely

00:53:04.626 --> 00:53:08.550
rational acts of these people.

00:53:08.550 --> 00:53:12.060
But if you ask about identity,
it's more complicated.

00:53:12.060 --> 00:53:15.540
And with my pleasure,
after my presentation,

00:53:15.540 --> 00:53:18.570
I will share my text, we
write with Oksana Shevel,

00:53:18.570 --> 00:53:23.440
common text about the dynamic
of Ukrainian identities

00:53:23.440 --> 00:53:25.634
and it's a very long conversation.

00:53:25.634 --> 00:53:28.470
Because we have a different combination.

00:53:28.470 --> 00:53:30.900
And we write only about national identity

00:53:30.900 --> 00:53:35.044
not about the whole
hierarchy of identities,

00:53:35.044 --> 00:53:38.333
only about ethnic and national identities

00:53:38.333 --> 00:53:40.277
and you have here so many problems.

00:53:40.277 --> 00:53:44.150
When people for example,
ask about their nationality

00:53:44.150 --> 00:53:47.193
in Ukraine, they usually
don't know what they mean.

00:53:47.193 --> 00:53:51.780
They talk about citizenship
or they talk about ethnicity

00:53:51.780 --> 00:53:54.644
because we think this problem
from the Soviet states.

00:53:54.644 --> 00:53:58.530
It was a prescription statuses

00:53:58.530 --> 00:54:03.180
and in this case when someone from Ukraine

00:54:03.180 --> 00:54:08.180
says "I'm Ukrainian", you
don't what the person mean.

00:54:08.820 --> 00:54:11.170
It's about ethnicity or
it's about citizenship.

00:54:12.150 --> 00:54:13.830
We write about everything in detail.

00:54:13.830 --> 00:54:15.450
I will be happy to share this.

00:54:15.450 --> 00:54:17.880
It's really very interesting
to look on this dynamic

00:54:17.880 --> 00:54:21.240
because if we use quantitative data,

00:54:21.240 --> 00:54:22.860
it looks very optimistic,

00:54:22.860 --> 00:54:25.530
that we have so many
people who are Ukrainians.

00:54:25.530 --> 00:54:29.400
But when we start talking
about their real identity,

00:54:29.400 --> 00:54:31.533
how they construct their identity,

00:54:31.533 --> 00:54:32.812
absolutely as a feature,

00:54:32.812 --> 00:54:35.130
we found a very good way how
to ask people about this.

00:54:35.130 --> 00:54:38.635
First of all we ask them,

00:54:38.635 --> 00:54:40.410
"Are you thinking about you

00:54:40.410 --> 00:54:44.460
in the ethnic or national coordinates?"

00:54:44.460 --> 00:54:45.897
And many people says "yes".

00:54:46.890 --> 00:54:50.954
Only a small group says "no,
I don't want to talk about me

00:54:50.954 --> 00:54:54.177
as representative of some ethnic group."

00:54:54.177 --> 00:54:56.880
But these groupings are
very interesting too

00:54:56.880 --> 00:55:00.993
because it's the group
which are maybe not agreeing

00:55:00.993 --> 00:55:03.420
with official discourses,

00:55:03.420 --> 00:55:07.380
but they live in situation
of self-limitation,

00:55:07.380 --> 00:55:09.150
self-censorship.

00:55:09.150 --> 00:55:13.260
And why they avoid this
answer about their ethnicity.

00:55:13.260 --> 00:55:16.820
It's very interesting. Small
group, but very interesting.

00:55:16.820 --> 00:55:19.861
Other group, when we talk on the numbers,

00:55:19.861 --> 00:55:21.280
it looks very optimistic.

00:55:21.280 --> 00:55:22.800
But how can we ask?

00:55:22.800 --> 00:55:25.227
We ask first of all, how
do you identify yourself

00:55:25.227 --> 00:55:28.940
by ethnic or citizen, et cetera?

00:55:28.940 --> 00:55:31.710
And after that, when person use something,

00:55:31.710 --> 00:55:34.421
if I ask "Who are you by nationality?"

00:55:34.421 --> 00:55:38.120
And the person says, "I'm
a Ukrainian or Russian."

00:55:38.120 --> 00:55:40.650
And after this ask "What
does this mean for you?"

00:55:40.650 --> 00:55:45.650
And starting from this point,
the person start create

00:55:45.684 --> 00:55:49.710
this very interesting construct
of being national identity.

00:55:49.710 --> 00:55:52.673
And after that, we try
to systematize all these

00:55:53.863 --> 00:55:56.223
version of national identities.

00:55:57.360 --> 00:55:58.770
It's really very interesting

00:55:58.770 --> 00:56:03.300
but when you read everything

00:56:03.300 --> 00:56:05.340
I understand that it's not so easy

00:56:05.340 --> 00:56:09.103
like it's look like, when we
ask only about nationality.

00:56:11.590 --> 00:56:12.423
Thank you.

00:56:14.055 --> 00:56:15.750
- [Isabella] Hi I'm Isabella.

00:56:15.750 --> 00:56:17.160
I teach political science here.

00:56:17.160 --> 00:56:18.900
So my question actually builds on this,

00:56:18.900 --> 00:56:20.520
which is about the use of language.

00:56:20.520 --> 00:56:23.553
So, what language do they use?

00:56:24.390 --> 00:56:26.910
Not just by the internally
displaced persons,

00:56:26.910 --> 00:56:30.420
but by the officials, and
both by the volunteers

00:56:30.420 --> 00:56:31.817
and by the state (indistinct).

00:56:33.263 --> 00:56:36.096
As you observe their interactions.

00:56:37.850 --> 00:56:39.700
- You ask me about language as a country

00:56:39.700 --> 00:56:40.587
or about language for research?

00:56:40.587 --> 00:56:44.040
- [Isabella] No, language
when they talk to each other,

00:56:44.040 --> 00:56:44.873
when they interact.

00:56:44.873 --> 00:56:45.706
- In Ukraine.

00:56:47.970 --> 00:56:50.586
As I says, we really now perceive

00:56:50.586 --> 00:56:55.583
a very rapid Ukrainization in the country.

00:56:55.583 --> 00:57:00.527
It's really very hard to
separate how to identify,

00:57:01.863 --> 00:57:06.273
their, not answers, but us in this case.

00:57:07.140 --> 00:57:09.480
For example, I come back to this idea

00:57:09.480 --> 00:57:12.881
that I work, not with numbers,
but I work with senses,

00:57:12.881 --> 00:57:14.070
when I talk with people.

00:57:14.070 --> 00:57:15.240
What's very interesting for me,

00:57:15.240 --> 00:57:19.500
when I talk with my
interlocutor about this issue,

00:57:19.500 --> 00:57:22.623
how he perceives
Russian-speaking people now

00:57:22.623 --> 00:57:25.863
in the context of full scale aggression.

00:57:27.000 --> 00:57:30.297
He says me, that it's
very important for him

00:57:30.297 --> 00:57:33.000
the person start from something,

00:57:33.000 --> 00:57:36.510
from small sentences
in Ukrainian language.

00:57:36.510 --> 00:57:40.380
Because he needs this for
identification processes.

00:57:40.380 --> 00:57:44.760
And after that they know,
that these persons are ours,

00:57:44.760 --> 00:57:46.410
it's not others.

00:57:46.410 --> 00:57:50.130
And after that, many people
start talking Russian language,

00:57:50.130 --> 00:57:55.130
but now many of them try to
turn the language of Ukrainian.

00:57:55.230 --> 00:57:56.670
But it usually take time.

00:57:56.670 --> 00:57:57.780
It usually takes time.

00:57:57.780 --> 00:58:01.620
It's not possible to start
talking in other languages

00:58:01.620 --> 00:58:03.840
but all Ukrainians are bilingual.

00:58:03.840 --> 00:58:07.710
We have not in Ukraine, people
who know only one language.

00:58:07.710 --> 00:58:09.480
All Ukrainians bilingual.

00:58:09.480 --> 00:58:11.490
It's a question of practices.

00:58:11.490 --> 00:58:13.740
Some part of Ukraine have practices

00:58:13.740 --> 00:58:16.650
to use Russian in everyday life.

00:58:16.650 --> 00:58:19.500
Some other parts of Ukraine,
very actively use Ukrainian

00:58:19.500 --> 00:58:20.357
in everyday life.

00:58:20.357 --> 00:58:23.520
But in our interview we see
a very interesting moment

00:58:23.520 --> 00:58:26.580
which show how these

00:58:26.580 --> 00:58:29.030
protests started after 2014.

00:58:29.886 --> 00:58:32.880
For example, I have an
interview with a person

00:58:32.880 --> 00:58:36.903
who voluntarily joined
to military formation

00:58:36.903 --> 00:58:40.741
on the territory of
Donetsk and Lugansk oblask.

00:58:40.741 --> 00:58:45.090
When I ask him about the language usage,

00:58:45.090 --> 00:58:49.227
he says, it's very
important how he related it,

00:58:49.227 --> 00:58:51.045
"unfortunately Russian."

00:58:51.045 --> 00:58:52.316
(audience laughs)

00:58:52.316 --> 00:58:54.660
This "unfortunately Russian" shows you

00:58:54.660 --> 00:58:56.130
the perception of the situation

00:58:56.130 --> 00:58:59.587
and wishes to use the language.

00:59:00.751 --> 00:59:05.751
In 2018, I conducted research
in four oblast of Ukraine,

00:59:06.750 --> 00:59:08.491
it's Donetsk and Lugansk oblast,

00:59:08.491 --> 00:59:10.800
and the Zaporozhye and Kherson oblast.

00:59:10.800 --> 00:59:14.640
Now you see it's a battlefield in Ukraine.

00:59:14.640 --> 00:59:16.770
And of course, we ask about identity

00:59:16.770 --> 00:59:20.907
and we ask about language usage

00:59:20.907 --> 00:59:24.723
and what I found during this research.

00:59:26.400 --> 00:59:29.910
Unfortunately, it's not possible
to quickly find this here,

00:59:29.910 --> 00:59:31.923
but if I'm not mistake,

00:59:33.120 --> 00:59:35.370
when I ask about this,

00:59:35.370 --> 00:59:38.389
people who live in Donetsk
and Lugansk oblast,

00:59:38.389 --> 00:59:41.310
most of them, near 40% of them,

00:59:41.310 --> 00:59:46.310
says that their native
language is Ukraine.

00:59:47.520 --> 00:59:50.676
But when I ask, what language
do you use on the work,

00:59:50.676 --> 00:59:54.780
what language do you use
in everyday communication,

00:59:54.780 --> 00:59:59.270
it's usually Russian language.

00:59:59.270 --> 01:00:03.993
But here we see a desire to be Ukrainians.

01:00:05.586 --> 01:00:06.867
We come back to this
funny, "unfortunately"

01:00:08.741 --> 01:00:10.235
because it's the same.

01:00:10.235 --> 01:00:12.120
(people speak at the same time)

01:00:12.120 --> 01:00:13.530
- [Audience Member] Sorry,
what about the children

01:00:13.530 --> 01:00:18.360
are they bilingual from an early age,

01:00:18.360 --> 01:00:20.070
the curriculum is bilingual?

01:00:20.070 --> 01:00:22.642
- Especially children,
especially young person,

01:00:22.642 --> 01:00:25.140
because during 30 years in the Ukraine,

01:00:25.140 --> 01:00:27.510
we have this soft Ukrainization.

01:00:28.470 --> 01:00:33.470
For example, before 2014 in
my native city in Donetsk,

01:00:35.237 --> 01:00:38.651
I read a lecture only
in Ukrainian language

01:00:38.651 --> 01:00:42.060
and all our greeting was in Ukrainian.

01:00:42.060 --> 01:00:44.850
It was a state policy
and my young generation

01:00:44.850 --> 01:00:47.280
who study in Ukrainian school,

01:00:47.280 --> 01:00:48.780
study in Ukrainian university,

01:00:49.657 --> 01:00:52.527
have good practices to
use Ukrainian language.

01:00:52.527 --> 01:00:54.773
For many young people in
Ukraine, it's no problem

01:00:54.773 --> 01:00:57.510
to switch from Ukrainian to Russian.

01:00:57.510 --> 01:01:00.886
But not now, because
unfortunately Russian.

01:01:00.886 --> 01:01:03.060
Because it's political reaction now.

01:01:03.060 --> 01:01:05.070
Many Ukrainians know Russian very well

01:01:05.070 --> 01:01:07.320
but now they stop use language

01:01:07.320 --> 01:01:12.320
if they could change
the language in speech.

01:01:12.756 --> 01:01:14.220
- [Audience Member] So there's no coercion

01:01:14.220 --> 01:01:16.520
on the part of the Ukrainian state?

01:01:16.520 --> 01:01:18.920
So those that you observe, (indistinct).

01:01:22.890 --> 01:01:26.230
Was there pressure
towards Ukrainian persons?

01:01:27.710 --> 01:01:31.260
- No, I think it's a battle
for maybe these people

01:01:31.260 --> 01:01:32.670
and displaced persons.

01:01:32.670 --> 01:01:35.580
It's not a pressure.

01:01:35.580 --> 01:01:38.010
It's a process where you need to integrate

01:01:38.010 --> 01:01:41.160
this normal communication in the state.

01:01:41.160 --> 01:01:44.730
I think it's a conversation
about the right of people

01:01:44.730 --> 01:01:46.857
to talk in their native language.

01:01:46.857 --> 01:01:49.890
And for many Ukrainians,
Ukrainian is native language.

01:01:49.890 --> 01:01:51.960
But during long time we live

01:01:51.960 --> 01:01:54.093
in Russian-speaking environment.

01:01:55.200 --> 01:01:59.040
I think now, in my country,
we have these very important

01:01:59.040 --> 01:02:04.040
discussions between these
two different speaking group

01:02:05.070 --> 01:02:10.070
in Ukraine, how to have this compromise

01:02:11.820 --> 01:02:13.448
and according to the understanding

01:02:13.448 --> 01:02:15.948
of the rights of this country.

01:02:17.309 --> 01:02:19.680
I think it's a bold push
of everybody in Ukraine

01:02:19.680 --> 01:02:24.210
to start using the
Ukrainian in every day life.

01:02:25.363 --> 01:02:27.603
- [Marci] Maybe I'll
just ask one question,

01:02:29.010 --> 01:02:30.750
take advantage of my position.

01:02:30.750 --> 01:02:32.760
Since we have a couple more minutes.

01:02:32.760 --> 01:02:36.870
So, you had also talked
with pro-Russian people

01:02:36.870 --> 01:02:40.680
in the Donbas years ago
and with the rebels,

01:02:40.680 --> 01:02:41.970
what did you learn?

01:02:41.970 --> 01:02:44.340
What did you understand
about their position

01:02:44.340 --> 01:02:46.530
from having spoken with them?

01:02:46.530 --> 01:02:50.250
This is the point of view
that's so hard for us to get.

01:02:50.250 --> 01:02:55.250
Even now, I was pleading
with my friend Pablo Humanjek

01:02:55.440 --> 01:02:57.780
who's a young Polish
journalist, who covered the war

01:02:57.780 --> 01:03:00.390
in the Donbas and now
he's covering it again.

01:03:00.390 --> 01:03:02.760
He's very good at talking to people,

01:03:02.760 --> 01:03:05.550
he's like this young Polish guy.

01:03:05.550 --> 01:03:07.800
And I said, "Pablo,
can't you get interviews

01:03:07.800 --> 01:03:08.948
with Russian soldiers?

01:03:08.948 --> 01:03:11.783
If they're gonna talk to anyone."

01:03:11.783 --> 01:03:14.310
And he's like, "No, even I can't do it.

01:03:14.310 --> 01:03:16.590
I just can't talk to them."

01:03:16.590 --> 01:03:20.280
But what did you learn about
how people were thinking?

01:03:20.280 --> 01:03:22.063
- Thank you very much for this question.

01:03:22.063 --> 01:03:23.370
It's a very complicated question for me.

01:03:23.370 --> 01:03:26.985
I studied a lot, but I have
not answered this question.

01:03:26.985 --> 01:03:27.960
Clearly I have not

01:03:27.960 --> 01:03:29.850
a clear and simple
answer to this question.

01:03:29.850 --> 01:03:31.290
We work with motivation, for example,

01:03:31.290 --> 01:03:36.000
people who voluntarily join
this military formation,

01:03:36.000 --> 01:03:37.873
from the side of DPR and LPR.

01:03:39.150 --> 01:03:41.250
It was horrible research for me

01:03:41.250 --> 01:03:44.103
because I needed to talk
with people who are my enemy.

01:03:45.630 --> 01:03:47.760
Then I try to analyze the motivation

01:03:47.760 --> 01:03:51.120
and try to understand
what their motivation?

01:03:51.120 --> 01:03:54.783
Why they join this military
formation from other side?

01:03:56.100 --> 01:04:00.039
I think it's really very
important to understand

01:04:00.039 --> 01:04:04.130
that here it's not possible
to have a simple answer

01:04:04.130 --> 01:04:05.777
to this question and many of them

01:04:05.777 --> 01:04:09.510
have a long life strategy.

01:04:09.510 --> 01:04:13.119
Many of them was
unsuccessful during this life

01:04:13.119 --> 01:04:17.760
and this forms maybe negative perception

01:04:17.760 --> 01:04:22.760
of Ukrainian statehood which
resulted them in their choice.

01:04:23.830 --> 01:04:26.577
Then I work with these
flows of information

01:04:26.577 --> 01:04:28.743
and how people perceive the information.

01:04:30.150 --> 01:04:31.950
All historians know how it's look.

01:04:31.950 --> 01:04:36.330
It's a very simple for understanding this.

01:04:36.330 --> 01:04:39.851
For example, during this
initial stage in 2014

01:04:39.851 --> 01:04:41.620
and 2015 all people who
live on the territory

01:04:41.620 --> 01:04:46.500
of DPR and LPR have
free access to internet.

01:04:46.500 --> 01:04:49.050
But when you have this
free access to internet

01:04:49.050 --> 01:04:52.500
why you not diversify the information,

01:04:52.500 --> 01:04:55.560
why you don't feed the other sources?

01:04:55.560 --> 01:04:57.690
No, many people don't
feed and how it's look,

01:04:57.690 --> 01:04:59.790
it's usually look in a very simple way.

01:04:59.790 --> 01:05:04.189
You start with some text and
see that it's opposite to you,

01:05:04.189 --> 01:05:05.847
you will not agree with this text

01:05:05.847 --> 01:05:07.620
and you close these sources

01:05:07.620 --> 01:05:10.350
and start to seek the other sources

01:05:10.350 --> 01:05:12.750
which are very close to your perception

01:05:12.750 --> 01:05:13.650
of the real situation.

01:05:13.650 --> 01:05:16.493
And after that, we see how this audience

01:05:16.493 --> 01:05:19.230
encapsulated during this stage

01:05:19.230 --> 01:05:22.650
and the distance between
them become very big

01:05:22.650 --> 01:05:27.650
and they oriented to absolutely
other model of perception

01:05:27.870 --> 01:05:30.180
of situation, model of
behavior, et cetera.

01:05:30.180 --> 01:05:31.740
So many reasons.

01:05:31.740 --> 01:05:33.902
So many reasons and we
see from the point of view

01:05:33.902 --> 01:05:37.710
of childhood, from the point
of view they are successful

01:05:37.710 --> 01:05:39.310
when they start their education.

01:05:40.200 --> 01:05:42.000
For example, it was
very interesting for me,

01:05:42.000 --> 01:05:45.963
many of our interlocutors from
that side of the conflict,

01:05:47.700 --> 01:05:51.360
says about the educational strategy

01:05:51.360 --> 01:05:55.470
like very specific, for
example, it's combination

01:05:55.470 --> 01:05:57.020
of ideotechnic and culturology.

01:06:00.296 --> 01:06:03.450
It's the person trying to find themselves

01:06:03.450 --> 01:06:05.520
in absolutely different spheres

01:06:05.520 --> 01:06:08.599
and as a result have not any good result

01:06:08.599 --> 01:06:13.599
for their successful life,
for example, in this phase.

01:06:15.440 --> 01:06:18.030
It's really so many, so many moments

01:06:18.030 --> 01:06:20.543
which are important to
understand in how it's working.

01:06:25.380 --> 01:06:28.203
- [Audience Member] Hi, I'm
Elian, based doing research

01:06:28.203 --> 01:06:32.250
from the Refugees Study
Center in Oxford in the UK.

01:06:32.250 --> 01:06:34.559
I wanted to ask, because
you were talking a bit about

01:06:34.559 --> 01:06:37.570
younger people who were choosing not to

01:06:39.660 --> 01:06:44.130
take IDP status, I think you
said, because of some stigma,

01:06:44.130 --> 01:06:45.660
or not wanting to be
a burden on the state,

01:06:45.660 --> 01:06:48.600
I wonder if you could
explain more about why

01:06:48.600 --> 01:06:52.800
they felt that that status
was something stigmatized.

01:06:52.800 --> 01:06:56.130
And also about what they do instead,

01:06:56.130 --> 01:06:57.810
how they would identify instead?

01:06:57.810 --> 01:07:02.583
Or how they would go
about making themselves?

01:07:05.280 --> 01:07:06.113
- Thank you very much.

01:07:06.113 --> 01:07:08.910
It was one of the set of IDPs
which was very interesting

01:07:08.910 --> 01:07:11.370
to understand and why they
choose such strategies

01:07:11.370 --> 01:07:12.810
when they relocated.

01:07:12.810 --> 01:07:16.440
Many young people and I
think it's main difference

01:07:16.440 --> 01:07:18.060
between young and old generation.

01:07:18.060 --> 01:07:20.520
Many young people haven't experienced

01:07:20.520 --> 01:07:25.520
how to run the house, before
their forced relocation

01:07:26.010 --> 01:07:29.493
and they not be afraid of
the future in this case.

01:07:29.493 --> 01:07:32.160
They come from Donetsk
and Lugansk region to Kyiv

01:07:32.160 --> 01:07:34.200
and start their new career.

01:07:34.200 --> 01:07:37.977
But while we talk about this strategy

01:07:37.977 --> 01:07:39.720
how to avoid the stigmatization

01:07:39.720 --> 01:07:41.870
because it's the
beginning of the conflict.

01:07:43.170 --> 01:07:44.847
Many people in Ukraine

01:07:46.640 --> 01:07:48.780
don't understand clearly what's happened.

01:07:48.780 --> 01:07:51.210
And I talk about this situation

01:07:51.210 --> 01:07:55.110
on the state-level called
anti-terroristic operation.

01:07:55.110 --> 01:07:57.420
And it's created a situation in society

01:07:57.420 --> 01:08:00.540
when IDPs perceived like collaborators,

01:08:00.540 --> 01:08:05.015
like people who are
invite Russian soldiers

01:08:05.015 --> 01:08:06.570
to Ukrainian land, et cetera, et cetera.

01:08:06.570 --> 01:08:10.579
And why young people have this opportunity

01:08:10.579 --> 01:08:14.310
and try to avoid to
receive these IDP statuses

01:08:14.310 --> 01:08:18.543
because of these ambiguous
perception in the society.

01:08:19.951 --> 01:08:21.570
It really was much more easy for them

01:08:21.570 --> 01:08:23.370
because they have previous experience

01:08:23.370 --> 01:08:26.433
how to weave his own pants,
how to lengthen the trousers.

01:08:29.177 --> 01:08:33.289
Many IDPs still these
document of those IDP statuses

01:08:33.289 --> 01:08:36.123
when they need to have
this help from the state.

01:08:38.070 --> 01:08:40.030
But then it's become

01:08:43.770 --> 01:08:48.210
the next stage, all of us
need to have this document,

01:08:48.210 --> 01:08:49.513
that we have status of IDPs

01:08:49.513 --> 01:08:50.910
because of other reasons.

01:08:50.910 --> 01:08:55.910
For example, when I try to
do something in official way,

01:08:57.300 --> 01:09:00.720
my passport, it's not enough
for solving many problems,

01:09:00.720 --> 01:09:02.670
I need to have this additional document

01:09:02.670 --> 01:09:04.524
about my IDP status.

01:09:04.524 --> 01:09:06.180
But now it's totally changed.

01:09:06.180 --> 01:09:09.723
Now our state talk about
these canceling of IDP status.

01:09:11.190 --> 01:09:14.010
But on the other hand, we
still need these statuses

01:09:14.010 --> 01:09:16.740
because its still about
the help of the people

01:09:16.740 --> 01:09:20.297
who are proceed the war,
who need the state help.

01:09:20.297 --> 01:09:22.170
It's a question of discussion in Ukraine.

01:09:22.170 --> 01:09:24.033
It's like how to be with the status.

01:09:24.033 --> 01:09:25.823
What it means now to be an IDP

01:09:25.823 --> 01:09:29.327
and how to deal with this
status in Ukrainian society now.

01:09:35.730 --> 01:09:36.570
- [Caroline] My name is Caroline,

01:09:36.570 --> 01:09:38.190
I'm a second year masters student

01:09:38.190 --> 01:09:40.710
in Russian States program.

01:09:40.710 --> 01:09:43.530
I have a question about the perceptions

01:09:43.530 --> 01:09:45.693
of Ukrainians abroad.

01:09:49.243 --> 01:09:51.960
I was in Poland over
the summer helping out

01:09:51.960 --> 01:09:54.843
at a reception point for
refugees from the Ukraine.

01:09:57.990 --> 01:10:01.123
I've been in Kyiv before.

01:10:01.123 --> 01:10:04.610
And you know, you saw just
anecdotally on the Kyiv metro,

01:10:05.550 --> 01:10:09.390
you'd see all these signs
advertising work in Poland.

01:10:09.390 --> 01:10:11.160
Someone told me before I came to Poland

01:10:11.160 --> 01:10:15.930
that before the war a lot
of Uber drivers in Poland

01:10:15.930 --> 01:10:20.550
were Ukrainian men and
then when the full scale

01:10:20.550 --> 01:10:22.380
aggression began, that people left

01:10:22.380 --> 01:10:27.380
and that created a shift
demographically in Poland,

01:10:28.350 --> 01:10:31.327
who was working, who was not.

01:10:32.190 --> 01:10:34.426
Have you seen any, in terms of,

01:10:34.426 --> 01:10:39.210
and then just walking around,
a significant outpouring

01:10:39.210 --> 01:10:42.390
of support for Ukrainian refugees

01:10:42.390 --> 01:10:45.723
and how people put aside businesses,

01:10:46.560 --> 01:10:48.210
their livelihoods,

01:10:48.210 --> 01:10:50.610
to devote significant
portions of their time

01:10:50.610 --> 01:10:53.310
to helping people coming in.

01:10:53.310 --> 01:10:57.760
What have you noticed in terms

01:10:59.175 --> 01:11:00.300
of the evolution of the perceptions

01:11:00.300 --> 01:11:03.000
of Ukrainians abroad over
time and how that's evolved?

01:11:05.160 --> 01:11:06.570
- Thank you.

01:11:06.570 --> 01:11:09.870
It's about current situation.
Thank you very much.

01:11:09.870 --> 01:11:11.730
I know much more about
the situation in Germany

01:11:11.730 --> 01:11:16.260
because I lived in Germany
and helped many refugees

01:11:16.260 --> 01:11:18.180
from Ukraine during this period.

01:11:18.180 --> 01:11:21.727
And very involved in these
volunteering procedures

01:11:21.727 --> 01:11:23.700
in Germany, I know how it's organized

01:11:23.700 --> 01:11:25.560
and how it looks in Germany.

01:11:25.560 --> 01:11:27.213
Perception was positive.

01:11:28.487 --> 01:11:30.660
Of course in Poland too.

01:11:30.660 --> 01:11:32.880
For me, it was unexpected level of support

01:11:32.880 --> 01:11:34.263
from Poland society.

01:11:34.263 --> 01:11:36.513
It's really very high level of support.

01:11:37.703 --> 01:11:41.880
We need to know that it's not state,

01:11:41.880 --> 01:11:44.858
first of all, support from
the level of the society,

01:11:44.858 --> 01:11:46.587
Polish society.

01:11:46.587 --> 01:11:50.820
Polish people are very actively
reacting to the situation.

01:11:50.820 --> 01:11:52.950
It's my personal situation,

01:11:52.950 --> 01:11:55.497
in the day when full scale war started

01:11:55.497 --> 01:11:58.110
I was in Berlin, with my youngest son,

01:11:58.110 --> 01:12:01.470
and I went by feet across the border

01:12:01.470 --> 01:12:06.210
from Shehyni, it's oblast to Poland.

01:12:06.210 --> 01:12:10.080
It was a first night after
the starting of full-scale war

01:12:10.080 --> 01:12:15.080
and people from Poland waiting for us

01:12:15.330 --> 01:12:17.223
on the Polish side of the border.

01:12:18.090 --> 01:12:20.310
It's first night.

01:12:20.310 --> 01:12:22.830
They come to the border and pick up people

01:12:22.830 --> 01:12:26.103
and help with any question.

01:12:27.270 --> 01:12:29.793
I think it's very high level of support.

01:12:31.774 --> 01:12:33.600
It still exists in Polish society.

01:12:33.600 --> 01:12:34.896
They actively involved.

01:12:34.896 --> 01:12:39.896
Of course, we're still faced
with some negative perception

01:12:39.900 --> 01:12:42.330
which we inherited from
the previous period.

01:12:42.330 --> 01:12:45.110
But it's something very human.

01:12:45.110 --> 01:12:47.158
We usually have different people

01:12:47.158 --> 01:12:49.198
who reacted in different way.

01:12:49.198 --> 01:12:51.810
But if you look on this situation at all,

01:12:51.810 --> 01:12:55.636
I think we have unprecedented level

01:12:55.636 --> 01:12:58.136
of help and support in Europe.

01:13:03.385 --> 01:13:04.513
- [Audience Member] Thanks
for your presentation.

01:13:04.513 --> 01:13:05.700
I have a question in terms

01:13:05.700 --> 01:13:09.120
of the joining the army,

01:13:09.120 --> 01:13:11.873
so a younger section of the IDPs.

01:13:11.873 --> 01:13:14.725
In just a comparison between
the younger section of IDP

01:13:14.725 --> 01:13:16.684
and the rest of the people.

01:13:16.684 --> 01:13:19.080
Young people in Ukraine
joining the war after 2014

01:13:19.080 --> 01:13:22.050
are joining the army after 2014

01:13:22.050 --> 01:13:24.240
and also more recently

01:13:24.240 --> 01:13:26.820
do you see some sort of
variation in terms of,

01:13:26.820 --> 01:13:29.670
for example, bureaucratic
hurdles for them to join the army

01:13:29.670 --> 01:13:31.803
because of the identity, various,

01:13:32.669 --> 01:13:35.850
and secondly, it's also what's
their intrinsic motivation

01:13:35.850 --> 01:13:37.350
in terms of serving the state.

01:13:39.750 --> 01:13:43.803
Have you seen any evidence
or insight about it?

01:13:44.760 --> 01:13:48.783
- Unfortunately I have not
any research about this.

01:13:49.930 --> 01:13:50.880
It's maybe only my,

01:13:52.587 --> 01:13:55.323
my feeling, my understanding of this.

01:13:58.349 --> 01:14:00.449
When we work with this motivation in 2014,

01:14:01.283 --> 01:14:03.090
because we have these two group,

01:14:03.090 --> 01:14:05.583
the one side the people who
support this DPR and LPR

01:14:06.507 --> 01:14:09.450
and on the other side, of
course, Ukrainian volunteers.

01:14:09.450 --> 01:14:11.970
And it was really very interesting to work

01:14:11.970 --> 01:14:16.080
on this motivation and many of them who

01:14:16.080 --> 01:14:19.380
voluntarily involved in such fighting,

01:14:19.380 --> 01:14:23.073
again in this first stage of the war,

01:14:24.167 --> 01:14:28.167
have a very clear description
of their identity.

01:14:30.083 --> 01:14:33.033
What I mean when I start
talking about this?

01:14:34.290 --> 01:14:37.953
In this case, when you are
not sure in your identity,

01:14:39.327 --> 01:14:41.390
when I ask you about
your identity, you start

01:14:42.353 --> 01:14:46.563
a very long and very detailed description

01:14:46.563 --> 01:14:49.320
how it's possible to identify you

01:14:49.320 --> 01:14:51.510
in different situations,
et cetera, et cetera.

01:14:51.510 --> 01:14:54.447
If people sure in their
identity, it's usually very clear

01:14:54.447 --> 01:14:56.797
and very simple answer, "I'm Ukrainian."

01:14:56.797 --> 01:15:00.207
"I'm citizen of Ukraine, it's
my country, it's my duty."

01:15:01.290 --> 01:15:03.810
It was very interesting to
compile this description

01:15:03.810 --> 01:15:07.950
of identity from these
people who supported other

01:15:07.950 --> 01:15:12.000
and people who joined this
Ukrainian volunteer formation.

01:15:12.000 --> 01:15:14.280
And I think it's the
same situation maybe now,

01:15:14.280 --> 01:15:17.850
in present day, when many young men,

01:15:17.850 --> 01:15:21.000
not young only, not only men in Ukraine,

01:15:21.000 --> 01:15:22.890
voluntarily join army.

01:15:22.890 --> 01:15:24.997
And I think it's a very strong motivation

01:15:24.997 --> 01:15:29.280
which argument of model
of political nation.

01:15:29.280 --> 01:15:32.049
I talk about this today,
they think now Ukrainians

01:15:32.049 --> 01:15:34.315
really perceive themselves
like political nation.

01:15:34.315 --> 01:15:36.077
It's a very important role.

01:15:36.077 --> 01:15:39.840
And all Ukrainians understand
that we are talking about

01:15:39.840 --> 01:15:41.587
our future in this situation.

01:15:41.587 --> 01:15:43.950
It's our duty to save our future.

01:15:43.950 --> 01:15:48.270
I think it's very strong
and main motivation

01:15:48.270 --> 01:15:52.593
for many our people who now join army.

01:15:54.660 --> 01:15:56.523
- [Marci] Please join me.

01:15:57.810 --> 01:15:59.430
I want to thank Prof. Mikheieva.

01:15:59.430 --> 01:16:01.230
I think there's still food out there

01:16:01.230 --> 01:16:03.150
so I'm going to do my Jewish mother thing

01:16:03.150 --> 01:16:06.093
and encourage everyone to eat
your protein and vegetables.

01:16:08.010 --> 01:16:09.660
Briefly in response to your question

01:16:09.660 --> 01:16:12.090
and for the non-Slavicists here.

01:16:12.090 --> 01:16:15.240
There is a novel that
recently has been translated

01:16:15.240 --> 01:16:19.710
to English, by one of Oksana's colleagues,

01:16:19.710 --> 01:16:21.723
Volodymyr Rafeyenko, from Donetsk,

01:16:22.687 --> 01:16:25.110
he was an internally displaced person.

01:16:25.110 --> 01:16:28.800
He moved in 2014, a great novelist

01:16:28.800 --> 01:16:31.050
who had been writing in
Russian, learned Ukrainian

01:16:31.050 --> 01:16:33.570
and wrote this novel "Mondegreen"

01:16:33.570 --> 01:16:37.650
about the experience of
being a displaced person

01:16:37.650 --> 01:16:41.100
and reconstructing a self
in a language that was his

01:16:41.100 --> 01:16:42.333
but wasn't quite his.

01:16:43.290 --> 01:16:45.750
And it was wonderfully
translated into English

01:16:45.750 --> 01:16:49.380
by my colleague, Mark
Andryczyk, at Columbia

01:16:49.380 --> 01:16:51.330
and it's just come out.

01:16:51.330 --> 01:16:54.120
If you want to commune
with that experience

01:16:54.120 --> 01:16:56.790
from the point of view of literary fiction

01:16:56.790 --> 01:16:58.533
I encourage to do that.

01:16:59.700 --> 01:17:02.580
Students please free to hang around

01:17:02.580 --> 01:17:04.256
and talk to Prof. Mikheieva,

01:17:04.256 --> 01:17:07.020
you can see she's very
friendly and approachable.

01:17:07.020 --> 01:17:08.735
And please join me in thanking her.

01:17:08.735 --> 01:17:11.735
(audience applauds)

